5.05.010","issn":["00034967","14682060"],"issn_org":["00034967"],"issue":"9","journal":"Annals of the Rheumatic Diseases","pages":"1528-1537","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Annals of the Rheumatic Diseases","volume":"84"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105008110981"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["E-Published (ahead of print)","Published"]},"sid":["aau-67cd2613-b31b-45d5-97f6-522672f5aacf","au-4216b7b7-873a-4078-8a82-38f5b995483a"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Exploring the limit of image resolution for human expert classification of vascular ultrasound images in giant cell arteritis and healthy subjects : the GCA-US-AI project","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-822339","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background Second primary malignancies (SPMs) are a well-known, long-term complication of antineoplastic treatment. This nationwide cohort study examined the risk of non-myeloid SPMs in survivors of adult acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) treated with intensive chemotherapy and, in some cases, allogeneic stem cell transplantation (alloSCT), compared to a matched general population. Methods Patients with incident AML between 2000 and 2018, alive and aged 18–70 years two years after start of intensive chemotherapy, were included and matched 1:10 to comparators from the general Danish population on sex, age, and the Nordic Multimorbidity Index. Exclusion criteria were non-myeloid SPMs for both AML survivors and comparators. Findings A total of 750 AML survivors and 7500 comparators were followed for a median of 10.6 years. The hazard ratio (HR) of non-myeloid SPMs was 1.55 (95% confidence interval [CI] 1.27–1.89) for AML survivors compared to comparators, driven by non-melanoma skin cancer (HR 2.52, 95% CI 1.90–3.35), not of solid cancer (HR 1.14, 95% CI 0.87–1.49). The 10-year cumulative incidences of any non-myeloid SPM were 13.5% (95% CI 10.6–16.5%) in AML survivors and 11.9% (95% CI 11.1–12.8%) in matched comparators. Additionally, AML survivors consolidated with alloSCT had a higher hazard rate of non-myeloid SPMs compared to non-transplanted AML survivors (adjusted HR 1.50, 95% CI 1.00–2.26). Interpretation The increased rate of non-myeloid SPMs observed in this population-based cohort study of AML survivors was almost entirely driven by non-melanoma skin cancer and is thus outweighed by the importance of intensive chemotherapy. Funding Svend Andersen, Heinrich Kopps, and Karen Elise Jensen’s Foundation.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Nielsen, Nanna Nørtoft","Jensen, Jonas Faartoft","Baech, Joachim","Trab, Trine","El-Galaly, Tarec Christoffer","Schöllkopf, Claudia","Ørskov, Andreas Due","Ommen, Hans Beier","Granfeldt, Lene Sofie","Kristensen, Daniel Tuyet","Severinsen, Marianne Tang"],"cuid":["S04-099.93-792956"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"corresponding_author":["Severinsen, Marianne Tang"],"keyword":["Acute myeloid leukaemia","Allogeneic stem cell transplantation","Intensive chemotherapy","Long term toxicities","Second primary non myeloid malignancies"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"second primary non myeloid malignancies following intensive treatment for adult acute myeloid leukaemia a danish population based cohort study","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lanepe.2024.101204","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S04-099.93-792956","identifier":[{"id":"39844882","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC11750488","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85213271386","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-92512610-9cae-4519-a1cc-97a77fe70e48","au-3689b94f-ce47-4631-85b6-f10dd39e735c","ku-d885be81-22a9-41b5-9b09-8adf7f5f4828","sdu-aaf9b415-c232-48fe-a486-298fca53e3b7"],"keyword":["Acute myeloid leukaemia","Allogeneic stem-cell transplantation","Intensive chemotherapy","Long-term toxicities","Second primary non-myeloid malignancies"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-7-270488D06E9D72AC7F17E1E50485D39E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/769145465/Nielsen_et_al._2025_._Second_