{"abstract":"Background. While use of some older antiretroviral drugs (ARVs) is associated with chronic liver enzyme elevation (cLEE), the impact of newer ARVs remains unknown. Methods. People with HIV enrolled in the RESPOND cohort who started an ARV after January 1, 2012 were included (baseline). The primary outcome was first cLEE individuals were censored at first of cLEE, last visit, death, or December 31, 2021. Incidence rates (IRs; events/1000 person-years) were calculated for each ARV overall and by ARV exposure (6–12 months, 1–2 years, and 2+ years). Poisson regression was used to estimate the incidence rate ratio (IRR) of cLEE and its association with individual ARVs and ARV class. Results. Of 17 106 individuals included contributing 87 924 person-years of follow-up, 1932 (11.3%) experienced cLEE (incidence rate [IR], 22.0; 95% CI, 21.0–23.0). There was no evidence of a cumulative ARV effect on cLEE incidence, (6–12 months: IR, 45.8; 95% CI, 41.4–50.19; 1–2 years: IR, 34.3; 95% CI, 31.5–37.4; and 2+ years: IR, 18.5; 95% CI, 17.4–19.7). Any use (vs no prior use) of non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) as a class and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) was independently associated with an increased IRR of cLEE, and any use of darunavir (DRV) was associated with a decreased risk of cLEE. Conclusions. cLEE is common and more frequent during the first year after initiating new ARVs. With a >5-year median follow-up, we found no short-term liver safety concerns with the use of INSTIs. Use of NNRTIs and TDF was associated with an increased cLEE risk, while DRV was associated with lower risk.","author_count":19,"contributors":["Roen, Ashley O.","Peters, Lars","Wandeler, Gilles","van der Valk, Marc","Zangerle, Robert","Günthard, Huldrych F.","Wit, Ferdinand","Mussini, Cristina","De Wit, Stéphane","d’Arminio Monforte, Antonella","Vehreschild, Jörg Janne","Castagna, Antonella","Jaschinski, Nadine","Vannappagari, Vani","Chen, Linda","Tallada, Joan","C’mar, John","Mocroft, Amanda","Nielsen, Lene Ryom"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-755012"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","Belgium","Germany","Italy","Netherlands","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["ASST Santi Paolo e Carlo","All Global Affiliations","Amsterdam UMC","European AIDS Treatment Group","Gilead Sciences","GlaxoSmithKline","HIV Monitoring Foundation","Innsbruck Medical University","MSD","Saint-Pierre University Hospital","University College London","University Hospital Bern","University Hospital Zürich","University of Cologne","University of Modena and Reggio Emilia","University of Zurich","Vita-Salute San Raffaele University"],"keyword":["Art","HCV","HIV"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"chronic liver enzyme elevation and use of contemporary arvs among people with hiv","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/ofid/ofae308","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-755012","identifier":[{"id":"38919512","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85197893564","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC11196901","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-f292d347-460a-403d-87c4-786d6605472c"],"keyword":["ART","Chronic liver enzyme elevation (cLEE)","HCV","HIV"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-23BDC9FA1BF4BF9F0CD661C735EA4653"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/399159824/ofae308.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https