{"abstract":"Background: Delirium severity scores are gaining acceptance for measuring delirium in the intensive care unit (ICU). Objective: The aim of this study was to evaluate the concordance between the Confusion Assessment Method for the intensive care unit (CAM-ICU)-7 and the Intensive Care Delirium Screening Checklist (ICDSC) as delirium severity measurement tools. Methods: This was a prospective, comparative, observational multicentre study. This study was conducted in 18 Danish ICUs. Delirium was assessed in adult critically ill patients admitted to an ICU with a Richmond Agitation and Sedation Score (RASS) of −2 or above. ICU nurses assessed delirium with randomised paired delirium screening instruments, using the CAM-ICU, the ICDSC, and the CAM-ICU-7. The correlation between the CAM-ICU-7 and the ICDSC severity scores was evaluated for all predefined patient subgroups. Results: A total of 1126 paired screenings were conducted by 127 ICU nurses in 850 patients. The patients' median age was 70 years (interquartile range: 61–77), 40% (339/850) were female, and 54% (457/850) had at least one positive delirium score. Delirium severity ranges (CAM-ICU-7: 0–7; and ICDSC: 0–8) were positively correlated (Pearson's correlation coefficient, r = 0.83; p < 0.0001). The overall agreement between the CAM-ICU-7 and the ICDSC for delirium measurement (CAM-ICU-7: >2, and ICDSC: >3) was substantial (kappa = 0.74), but the agreement decreased to fair (kappa = 0.38) if a patient had a RASS less than 0. Conclusions: The agreement between the CAM-ICU-7 and the ICDSC for delirium severity measurement was substantial but might be affected by the patient's sedation and agitation level at the time of assessment. Implications for practice: Both CAM-ICU-7 and ICDSC can be implemented for delirium severity measurement. Attention is warranted in both scores if a patient has a RASS of −2.","author_count":21,"contributors":["Collet, Marie Oxenbøll","Nielsen, Anne Højager","Larsen, Laura Krone","Lærkner, Eva","Jensen, Janet Froulund","Mortensen, Camilla Bekker","Lehmkuhl, Lene","Thorn, Linette","Rasmussen, Birgitte Sonne","Allerslev, Tina","Laursen, Edel","Shiv, Louise Hvid","Villumsen, Marianne","Rahr, Mette Nygaard","Svenningsen, Helle"],"cuid":["S03-096.89-735252"],"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Collet, Marie O.","Collet, Marie Oxenbøll"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Critical Care methods","Critical Illness psychology","Critically ill","Delirium","Delirium diagnosis","Delirium severity","Female","Hospitalization","Humans","Intensive Care Units","Intensive care unit","Male","Prospective Studies","Screening method","disease health science and nursing"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-KEN","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-VIA"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-KEN","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-VIA"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"delirium and delirium severity screening in the intensive care correspondence of screenings tools","src":["au","sdu","via"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-VIA"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aucc.2023.05.007","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Statistical support was funded by Sygeplejerskernes Forskningsfond and by the VIA University College, Research Centre for Health and Welfare Technology. Helle Svenningsen received funding for the project from “Sygeplejerskernes Forskningsfond” and from the VIA University College, Research Centre for Health and Welfare Technology. Anne Højager Nielsen was funded by The NOVO Nordisk Foundation for another project. NNF19OC0058277. Marie collet was funded by The NOVO Nordisk Foundation for another project NNF 21OC0072048 The use of patient data for this study without patient consent was approved by the Danish Patient Safety Authority 31-1521-440 on 09-08-2020. An extension of the study was granted by Central Denmark Region 1-45-70-3-21 on 08-25-2021. The study named “Delirium Screening 3 Methods study” was registered with Central Denmark Region 1-16-02-546-20 on 09.09.2020.Statistical support was funded by Sygeplejerskernes Forskningsfond and by the VIA University College, Research Centre for Health and Welfare Technology."],"id":"S03-096.89-735252","identifier":[{"id":"37438182","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85165238883","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-88b9e2be-631f-4543-ad59-add627a0b0cc","sdu-3558339a-bb2a-4eee-8e22-7775099a411c","via-6bffc20e-3f6e-4416-9337-6ab3946c3e98"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Critical Care/methods","Critically ill","Delirium/diagnosis","Female","Hospitalization","Humans","Intensive Care Units","Intensive care unit","Male","Prospective Studies","critical illness/psychology","delirium","delirium severity","disease, health science and nursing","screening method"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-26C889B44A4CD85F819884B9C7E1CEC8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.australiancriticalcare.com/action/showPdf?pii=S1036-7314%2823%2900082-6","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aucc.2023.05.007","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"kennedy center","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-KEN","l3nn":"Kennedy Center","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Kennedy Center","namevar":["Kennedy Center"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-KEN","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"