{"author_count":4,"contributors":["Nielsen, Maja Hykkelbjerg","Seibæk, Lene","Pedersen, Michael Lynge","Jensen, Annesofie Lunde"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718183"],"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"i hope someone comes and tells us about diabetes must be nice to hear about it health literacy and self management of people living with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or type 000000000002 diabetes in greenland a qualitative interpretive description study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-718183","ids":["au-f20995bf-b173-49af-8de2-c3a5659f4fe3"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-7D9E76159FF70E2BA5D5CBE06D844872"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - SDCA-Steno Diabetes Center Aarhus"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Maja Hykkelbjerg","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"358f245d-ba50-4f0d-9292-c5c2b79ee16b","name":"Nielsen, Maja Hykkelbjerg","orcid":"0000-0003-1373-7661","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"seibækpau","last":"Seibæk","locid":"2bf95667-24b9-444b-b3e1-a62965aac67a","name":"Seibæk, Lene","orcid":"0000-0002-2396-8669","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael Lynge","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","name":"Pedersen, Michael Lynge","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Annesofie Lunde","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"337f63a9-307c-4851-9e53-cf0856200457","name":"Jensen, Annesofie Lunde","orcid":"0000-0003-2025-6663","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["14712458"],"journal":"BMC Public Health","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"s","title":"BMC Public Health"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Submitted"]},"sid":["au-f20995bf-b173-49af-8de2-c3a5659f4fe3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"“I hope someone comes and tells us about diabetes. Must be nice to hear about it.” Health literacy and self-management of people living with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or type 2 diabetes in Greenland – A qualitative interpretive description study\"","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718183","year":"2024","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: After anterior cruciate ligament reconstruction (ACL-R), a localised scar tissue called cyclops lesion may develop anterior to the graft causing knee extension deficits, pain, oedema, clicking and reduced knee function. This study determined the incidence of arthroscopic resection of a cyclops lesion within 2 years after ACL-R and investigated the associations of patient characteristics and surgical techniques with the need for arthroscopic resection of a cyclops lesion. METHODS: This study included patients who underwent primary ACL-R with adult surgical technique from 2005 to 2019 at Aarhus University Hospital, Denmark. The cohort was identified in a national registry. To identify patients who had resected a cyclops lesion within the first 2 years after ACL-R, patients' surgical records were reviewed. RESULTS: In 2005-2019, 2556 patients underwent primary ACL-R; 176 developed cyclops lesions that were resected within 2 years, equivalent to an incidence of 6.9% (95% confidence interval [CI]: 5.9-7.9). When stratified by the femoral drilling technique used, this incidence was 8.9% (95% CI: 7.7-10.3) with the anteromedial technique and 1.9% (95% CI: 1.0-3.1) with the transtibial technique. The incidence was 8.5% (95% CI: 6.8-10.3) in women and 5.7% (95% CI: 4.6-7.1) in men. Age, graft choice and the presence of cartilage or meniscal lesions did not affect the incidence. CONCLUSION: The overall incidence of a cyclops lesion removal within 2 years post-ACL-R was 6.9%. This was five times higher with the anteromedial femoral drilling technique than with the transtibial technique. Women had a 47% higher incidence of cyclops lesion removal than men. This is relevant for the surgeon when planning an ACL-R. LEVEL OF EVIDENCE: Level II.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Miller, Lene Lindberg","Lind, Martin","Mechlenburg, Inger","Nielsen, Torsten Grønbech"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-728343"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Miller, Lene Lindberg"],"keyword":["ACL","Adolescent","Adult","Anterior Cruciate Ligament Injuries surgery","Anterior Cruciate Ligament Reconstruction methods","Arthroscopy methods","Cicatrix etiology","Cohort Studies","Complication","Denmark epidemiology","Female","Humans","Incidence","Knee","Male","Middle Aged","Postoperative Complications epidemiology","Reconstruction","Retrospective Studies","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"seven percent of primary anterior crucial ligament reconstruction patients have arthroscopic resection of cyclops lesions within 000000000002 years a cohort study of 000000002556 patients","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85190971452","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-728343","identifier":[{"id":"85190971452","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38629753","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-bde4a491-0cef-4d5b-8122-747c7937fc78"],"keyword":["ACL","Adolescent","Adult","Anterior Cruciate Ligament Injuries/surgery","Anterior Cruciate Ligament Reconstruction/methods","Arthroscopy/methods","Cicatrix/etiology","Cohort Studies","Denmark/epidemiology","Female","Humans","Incidence","Male","Middle Aged","Postoperative Complications/epidemiology","Retrospective Studies","Young Adult","complication","cyclops lesion","knee","reconstruction"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F11268C0121F2A94FD20EA8A32BED10A"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Orthopaedic surgery"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lene Lindberg","key":"millerpau","last":"Miller","locid":"7e1b0171-af03-47af-bf29-00daeab6a85c","name":"Miller, Lene Lindberg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Martin","key":"lindpau","last":"Lind","locid":"5a1298e3-a4d7-4818-88f4-da6cdbf1784d","name":"Lind, Martin","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Inger","key":"mechlenburgpau","last":"Mechlenburg","locid":"b46c351d-ea23-4836-8426-a5402fd1a2bd","name":"Mechlenburg, Inger","orcid":"0000-0001-5432-8691","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Torsten Grønbech","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"33da3303-c376-4195-b3d6-7767956a26fe","name":"Nielsen, Torsten Grønbech","orcid":"0000-0001-5354-1781","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38629753"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/ksa.12165","issn":["09422056","14337347"],"issn_org":["09422056"],"issue":"6","journal":"Knee Surgery, Sports Traumatology, Arthroscopy","pages":"1455-1461","publisher":"John Wiley & Sons Inc.","status":"p","title":"Knee Surgery, Sports Traumatology, Arthroscopy","volume":"32"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85190971452"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-bde4a491-0cef-4d5b-8122-747c7937fc78"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Seven percent of primary anterior crucial ligament reconstruction patients have arthroscopic resection of cyclops lesions within 2 years : A cohort study of 2556 patients","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-728343","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Plant–fungal interactions are ubiquitous across ecosystems and contribute significantly to plant ecology and evolution. All orchids form obligate symbiotic relationships with specific fungi for germination and early growth, and the distribution of terrestrial orchid species has been linked to occurrence and abundance of specific orchid mycorrhizal fungi (OMF) in the soil. The availability of OMF can therefore be a habitat requirement that is relevant to consider when establishing management and conservation strategies for threatened orchid species, but knowledge on the spatial distribution of OMF in soil is limited. We here studied the mycorrhizal associations of three terrestrial orchid species (Anacamptis pyramidalis, Orchis purpurea and Platanthera chlorantha) found in a local orchid diversity hotspot in eastern Denmark, and investigated the abundance of the identified mycorrhizal fungi in the surrounding soil. We applied ITS metabarcoding to samples of orchid roots, rhizosphere soil and bulk soil collected at three localities, supplemented with standard barcoding of root samples with OMF specific primers, and detected 22 Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) putatively identified as OMF. The three orchid species displayed different patterns of OMF associations, supporting the theory that association with specific fungi constitutes part of an orchid's ecological niche allowing co-occurrence of many species in orchid-rich habitats. The identified mycorrhizal partners in the basidiomycete families Tulasnellaceae and Ceratobasidiaceae (Cantharallales) were detected in low abundance in rhizosphere soil, and appeared almost absent from bulk soil at the localities. This finding highlights our limited knowledge of the ecology and trophic mode of OMF outside orchid tissues, as well as challenges in the detection of specific OMF with standard methods. Potential implications for management and conservation strategies are discussed.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Hartvig, Ida","Kosawang, Chatchai","Rasmussen, Hanne Nina","Kjær, Erik Dahl","Nielsen, Lene Rostgaard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-731539"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Smithsonian Institution"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"co occurring orchid species associated with different low abundance mycorrhizal fungi from the soil in a high diversity conservation area in denmark","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.10863","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-731539","identifier":[{"id":"85183932446","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10828919","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"38304271","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-77c71c1d-030f-4859-ad9e-217ce8fa6c91"],"keyword":["Ceratobasidiaceae","ITS metabarcoding","Tulasnellaceae","orchid mycorrhizal fungi","plant hologenomics","soil mycobiome"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1631EE9CA75267AF5ADDBC4F4CAECF90"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/389307373/Ecology_and_Evolution_2024_Hartvig_Co_occurring_orchid_species_associated_with_different_low_abundance_mycorrhizal.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.10863","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Globe Institute, Section for Hologenomics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"smithsonian institution","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Smithsonian Institution","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Ida","key":"hartvigpau","last":"Hartvig","locid":"445ddaaa-d012-4915-9a41-a4428d87a592","name":"Hartvig, Ida","orcid":"0000-0002-1509-6864","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Chatchai","key":"kosawangpau","last":"Kosawang","locid":"1b69b056-6473-427b-81f9-20fbd6797849","name":"Kosawang, Chatchai","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hanne","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"3d9a5aa1-c232-468d-9b54-e24669fe718d","name":"Rasmussen, Hanne Nina","name_org":"Rasmussen, Hanne","orcid":"0000-0002-0181-9304","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Erik Dahl","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"e1bdaffb-0384-4159-aa20-a913a2cee948","name":"Kjær, Erik Dahl","orcid":"0000-0001-8624-1611","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lene Rostgaard","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"8e8b51ee-3f45-4ebe-b2fb-fa79f1256d08","name":"Nielsen, Lene Rostgaard","orcid":"0000-0002-7214-8691","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38304271"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/ece3.10863","issn":["20457758"],"issue":"2","journal":"Ecology and Evolution","paperid":"e10863","status":"p","title":"Ecology and Evolution","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85183932446"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-77c71c1d-030f-4859-ad9e-217ce8fa6c91"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Co-occurring orchid species associated with different low-abundance mycorrhizal fungi from the soil in a high-diversity conservation area in Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-731539","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Cytoreductive surgery (CRS) with hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy (HIPEC) has improved the 5-year survival for colorectal cancer (CRC) patients with peritoneal metastases (PM). Little is known about recurrence patterns and recurrence rates between synchronous (S) and metachronous (M) PM following CRS+HIPEC. We aimed to describe the recurrence patterns, overall survival (OS) and disease-free survival (DFS) in S-PM and M-PM patients after complete CRS+HIPEC. From June 2006 to December 2020, a prospective cohort study included 310 CRC patients, where 181 patients had S-PM (58.4%) and 129 patients had M-PM (41.6%). After a median 10.3-month follow-up, 247/310 (79.7%) patients experienced recurrence, and recurrence sites included isolated peritoneal (32.4%), multifocal (peritoneal and liver and/or lung(s)) (22.7%), isolated liver (17.8%), isolated lung (10.5%) and other (16.6%) sites. Recurrence patterns did not differ between S-PM and M-PM. M-PM patients had an impaired DFS compared to S-PM patients (9.4 months (95% CI: 7.3–12.1) vs. 12.5 months (95% CI: 11.2–13.9), p = 0.01). The median OS was similar for S-PM and M-PM (38.4 months (95% CI: 31.2–46.8) vs. 40.8 months (95% CI: 28.8–46.8), p = 0.86). Despite frequent recurrence at extraperitoneal locations, long-term survival was achievable after CRS+HIPEC in CRC patients with PM. The recurrence patterns and OS did not differ between groups, yet M-PM patients had a shorter DFS.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Nielsen, Mette Fugleberg","Ravn, Sissel","Sørensen, Mette Møller","Funder, Jonas Amstrup","Iversen, Lene Hjerrild"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-737900"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Iversen, Lene Hjerrild"],"keyword":["Colorectal cancer","Cytoreductive surgery","Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy","Prognosis","Recurrence","Synchronous peritoneal metastases"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"recurrence and survival following cytoreductive surgery and hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy for synchronous and metachronous peritoneal metastases of colorectal origin","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/cancers16030631","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-737900","identifier":[{"id":"85184673013","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38339382","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10854638","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-eeff5c00-5516-4f2b-a3ee-4a7748c329e7"],"keyword":["colorectal cancer","cytoreductive surgery","hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy","metachronous peritoneal metastases","prognosis","recurrence","synchronous peritoneal metastases"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-93F133C9B76C733FDEC1EDF0F971DDAA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/cancers16030631","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Surgical Gastroenterology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mette Fugleberg","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Mette Fugleberg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sissel","key":"ravnpau","last":"Ravn","locid":"28591e63-9eb6-4fa6-a369-9bf67b660910","name":"Ravn, Sissel","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mette Møller","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"447f876a-1fe6-4695-ac7b-36da85ce31ea","name":"Sørensen, Mette Møller","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jonas Amstrup","key":"funderpau","last":"Funder","locid":"f7ae2db6-a9b1-43c4-94a2-39f78f9202d1","name":"Funder, Jonas Amstrup","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lene Hjerrild","key":"iversenpau","last":"Iversen","locid":"4d8ada79-98c2-49eb-ac2f-472b41434eff","name":"Iversen, Lene Hjerrild","orcid":"0000-0002-2336-2923","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38339382"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/cancers16030631","issn":["20726694"],"issue":"3","journal":"Cancers","paperid":"631","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","status":"p","title":"Cancers","volume":"16"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85184673013"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-eeff5c00-5516-4f2b-a3ee-4a7748c329e7"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Recurrence and Survival Following Cytoreductive Surgery and Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy for Synchronous and Metachronous Peritoneal Metastases of Colorectal Origin","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-737900","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Despite the widely reported success of knee arthroplasty, studies show that 1.6–3 % of patients undergo revision within the first postoperative year predominantly due to infection. Preoperative skin preparation may potentially decrease the bacterial load and consequently, the risk of periprosthetic joint infections. The effects of hair removal on prosthetic joint infection are inconsistent. Our primary aim was to investigate if hair removal with a clipper influenced skin colonisation and bacterial composition. Methods: Forty Caucasian male participants who were planned to undergo knee arthroplasty, (mean age 63.8 years), were included. Patients were randomised to hair removal in a within-person study design. As a control, the opposite leg of the patient was used. Swabs were collected prior to hair removal (baseline), immediately after hair removal (Day 0), and with follow-up after one and seven days. Results: The intervention showed significant decrease in mean log colony-forming units per. cm2 from baseline 2.97 to 2.67 (P<0.01) immediately after hair removal and sustained at Day 1 (P=0.01). At Day 7, the mean was non-significant compared to baseline. The control group did not show any decrease of skin microbiota at follow-up on Day 0, 1 or 7. No significant differences within the bacterial composition were found between the intervention and control leg at baseline among the six most prevalent detected bacterial species: Staphylococcus epidermidis, Micrococcus luteus, S. hominis, S. capitis, S. haemolyticus and S. aureus. The study did not find any changes in the bacterial composition over time. Conclusion: Hair removal with a clipper within 24 hours prior to surgery causes a significant non-selective reduction in skin colonisation.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Hasløv, Trine Herskind","Fuglsbjerg, Cecilie","Nielsen, Anne Kirstine","Hesselvig, Anne Brun","Fritz, Blaine Gabriel","Bay, Lene","Møller, Tom","Bjarnsholt, Thomas","Odgaard, Anders"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-748462"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Hair Removal","Knee arthroplasty","Randomised controlled trial","Skin preparation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hair removal with a clipper and microbial colonisation prior to knee arthroplasty a randomised controlled trial","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infpip.2024.100377","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-748462","identifier":[{"id":"85196748830","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC11259930","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"39035677","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-80b4c098-f6df-4880-a33d-b8c096e2346d"],"keyword":["Clipper","Hair removal","Knee arthroplasty","Randomised controlled trial","Skin colonisation","Skin preparation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-54C1C0FDE190F47F605A18F9C72F07A4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infpip.2024.100377","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/396984970/1_s2.0_S2590088924000416_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Herlev and Gentofte"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Immunology and Microbiology, Bacteriology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Trine Herskind","key":"hasløvpau","last":"Hasløv","name":"Hasløv, Trine Herskind","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Cecilie","key":"fuglsbjergpau","last":"Fuglsbjerg","name":"Fuglsbjerg, Cecilie","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anne Kirstine","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"95809201-26e8-4cf6-b83d-38a815ebed25","name":"Nielsen, Anne Kirstine","orcid":"0000-0001-5652-8814","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anne Brun","key":"hesselvigpau","last":"Hesselvig","name":"Hesselvig, Anne Brun","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Blaine Gabriel","key":"fritzpau","last":"Fritz","locid":"c5199670-8c56-436d-ad51-c17f0a652973","name":"Fritz, Blaine Gabriel","orcid":"0000-0002-2085-894X","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Lene","key":"baypau","last":"Bay","locid":"22f49c41-e569-43c4-8978-94bc9e411869","name":"Bay, Lene","orcid":"0000-0002-4164-5900","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Tom","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","locid":"49fa461a-c0e0-4be7-b3a2-033bdf65caf2","name":"Møller, Tom","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Thomas","key":"bjarnsholtpau","last":"Bjarnsholt","locid":"cb3ccb7d-0415-40e5-9bba-c9bc26b6316d","name":"Bjarnsholt, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0002-8003-7414","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Anders","key":"odgaardpau","last":"Odgaard","locid":"33eeba1f-407c-4aa4-bf69-5efb12863f82","name":"Odgaard, Anders","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39035677"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.infpip.2024.100377","issn":["25900889"],"issue":"3","journal":"Infection Prevention in Practice","paperid":"100377","status":"p","title":"Infection Prevention in Practice","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85196748830"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-80b4c098-f6df-4880-a33d-b8c096e2346d"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Hair removal with a clipper and microbial colonisation prior to knee arthroplasty : a randomised controlled trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-748462","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Women with HIV are globally underrepresented in clinical research. Existing studies often focus on reproductive outcomes, seldom focus on older women, and are often underpowered to assess sex/gender differences. We describe CD4, HIV viral load (VL), clinical characteristics, comorbidity burden, and use of antiretroviral therapy (ART) among women with HIV in the RESPOND study and compare them with those of the men in RESPOND. Methods: RESPOND is a prospective, multi-cohort collaboration including over 34 000 people with HIV from across Europe and Australia. Demographic and clinical characteristics, including CD4/VL, comorbidity burden, and ART are presented at baseline, defined as the latter of 1 January 2012 or enrolment into the local cohort, stratified by age and sex/gender. We further stratify men by reported mode of HIV acquisition, men who have sex with men (MSM) and non-MSM. Results: Women account for 26.0% (n = 9019) of the cohort, with a median age of 42.2 years (interquartile range [IQR] 34.7–49.1). The majority (59.3%) of women were white, followed by 30.3% Black. Most women (75.8%) had acquired HIV heterosexually and 15.9% via injecting drug use. Nearly half (44.8%) were receiving a boosted protease inhibitor, 31.4% a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, and 7.8% an integrase strand transfer inhibitor. The baseline year was 2012 for 73.2% of women and >2019 for 4.2%. Median CD4 was 523 (IQR 350–722) cells/μl, and 73.6% of women had a VL <200 copies/mL. Among the ART-naïve population, women were more likely than MSM but less likely than non-MSM (p < 0.001) to have CD4 <200 cells/μL and less likely than both MSM and non-MSM (p < 0.001) to have VL ≥100 000 copies/mL. Women were also more likely to be free of comorbidity than were both MSM and non-MSM (p < 0.0001). Conclusion: RESPOND women are diverse in age, ethnicity/race, CD4/VL, and comorbidity burden, with important differences relative to men. This work highlights the importance of stratification by sex/gender for future research that may help improve screening and management guidelines specifically for women with HIV.","author_count":31,"contributors":["Hutchinson, J.","Neesgard, B.","Kowalska, J.","Grabmeier-Pfistershammer, K.","Johnson, M.","Kusejko, K.","De Wit, S.","Wit, F.","Mussini, C.","Castagna, A.","Stecher, M.","Pradier, C.","Domingo, P.","Carlander, C.","Wasmuth, J.","Chkhartishvili, N.","Uzdaviniene, V.","Haberl, A.","Monforte, A. d'Arminio","Garges, H.","Gallant, J.","Said, M.","Schmied, B.","van der Valk, M.","Konopnicki, D.","Jaschinski, N.","Mocroft, A.","Greenberg, L.","Burns, F.","Nielsen, Lene Ryom","Petoumenos, K."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-765055"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, Central","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","Austria","Belgium","France","Georgia","Germany","Italy","Lithuania","Netherlands","Poland","Spain","Sweden","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["AIDS and Clinical Immunology Research Center","ASST Santi Paolo e Carlo","All Global Affiliations","Free University of Brussels","Gilead Sciences","Goethe University Frankfurt","HIV Monitoring Foundation","Hospital de la Santa Creu i Sant Pau","Karolinska University Hospital","Medical University of Vienna","Medical University of Warsaw","Royal Free London NHS Foundation Trust","University College London","University Hospital Bonn","University of Amsterdam","University of Cologne","University of Côte d'Azur","University of Modena and Reggio Emilia","University of New South Wales","University of Vienna","University of Zurich","ViiV Healthcare","Vilnius University","Vita-Salute San Raffaele University"],"keyword":["Comorbidity","HIV","Respond","Women","antiretroviral therapy ART"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"clinical characteristics of women with hiv in the respond cohort a descriptive analysis and comparison to men","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-765055","identifier":[{"id":"85195413416","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC11563927","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"38840507","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-6c2dcb10-cfce-4691-89bc-3245eef4b824"],"keyword":["HIV","RESPOND","antiretroviral therapy (ART)","comorbidity","women"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-EB18619403BD8BF40B450C1F61243CE2"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"au","key":"university of new south wales","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"University of New South Wales","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"pl","key":"medical university of warsaw","loc":["Poland","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Medical University of Warsaw","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"at","key":"university of vienna","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Vienna","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"gb","key":"royal free london nhs foundation trust","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Royal Free London NHS Foundation Trust","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"ch","key":"university of zurich","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Zurich","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"be","key":"free university of brussels","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Free Uni