","corresponding":null,"first":"Robert","key":"skovrobertpau","last":"Skov","name":"Skov, Robert","rank":35,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"Kasper","key":"iversenkasperpau","last":"Iversen","locid":"558df261-ca9f-440a-8bee-7d38813c253f","name":"Iversen, Kasper","rank":36,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36546864"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1128/spectrum.04174-22","issn":["21650497"],"issue":"1","journal":"Microbiology Spectrum","paperid":"e0417422","publisher":"American Society for Microbiology","status":"p","title":"Microbiology Spectrum","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85148114069"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Roskilde University","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-77e38116-cf38-4495-aa26-eb61da156b99","au-86d3ec0c-bfd0-46b6-a5bc-f20eb4ad6e16","ku-d9b7bd1e-9f3b-4425-a86e-dfab5a04ba87","ruc-2305a9ec-adba-4137-a7bc-6dadbfc0e4bf","sdu-6970388d-8294-4828-a717-c6f65d30b736"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","RUC Roskilde University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"A Cross-Sectional Study of SARS-CoV-2 Antibodies and Risk Factors for Seropositivity in Staff in Day Care Facilities and Preschools in Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S05-098.37-005817","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"AIM: This paper describes the design of the 'Move More' study, which aims to develop and assess the feasibility of a social-prescribing intervention to increase physical activity among physically inactive Danes. BACKGROUND: Physical inactivity constitutes a public-health challenge in Denmark. Social prescribing may be a promising tool to tackle physical inactivity by linking physical activity support from general practitioners with community-based activities in sports clubs, as this may help physically inactive citizens become more physically active. Given the range of stakeholders and behaviours required for social prescribing of physical activity, an intervention that harnesses this approach may constitute a complex intervention. The methods and decisions made in the stages of developing complex interventions are seldom reported. The present study enabled us to describe how co-creation can be used in a pragmatic development process for a complex intervention that considers the needs of stakeholders and the conditions of the delivery context. METHODS: The study is based on the core elements of the development and feasibility phases of the Medical Research Council Framework for Developing and Evaluating Complex Interventions. Additionally, it is informed by a framework for the co-creation and prototyping of public-health interventions, drawing from a scoping review, stakeholder consultations and co-creation workshops. Ultimately, a feasibility study will be conducted to refine the programme theory by introducing the proposed intervention in case studies. PERSPECTIVES: The study will result in a prototype intervention manual and recommendations for implementation of an adapted social-prescribing intervention targeting physical inactivity in Denmark.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Lene Gissel","Nielsen, Rasmus Oestergaard","Kallestrup, Per","Hawkins, Jemma","Ryom, Knud"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-151992"],"filters":{"added":"2022-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Cardiff University"],"keyword":["Co creation","Community health services","Complex interventions","Exercise","Feasibility Studies","General practice","Health promotion","Humans","Physical activity","Referral and Consultation","Sedentary Behavior","Social prescribing","Sports"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"study design of move more development and feasibility of a social prescribing intervention to increase physical activity among inactive danes","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85131574219","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-151992","identifier":[{"id":"35656623","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85131574219","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-27795468-8ef6-4b81-9acf-773669aa8ce8"],"keyword":["Feasibility Studies","Humans","Sedentary Behavior","Social prescribing","Sports","co-creation","community health services","complex interventions","exercise","general practice","health promotion","physical activity","referral and consultation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CE4142D47D2330A2AF1A4F1A979D3837"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Public Health, Department of Public Health - Forskningsenheden for Almen Praksis"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"cardiff university","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Cardiff University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lene Gissel","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"09dbbcf4-a99a-4d25-a01c-800a7b767113","name":"Rasmussen, Lene Gissel","orcid":"0000-0002-7828-5697","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rasmus Oestergaard","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"2d1ea8cb-28f5-48df-baa8-9724c2e17005","name":"Nielsen, Rasmus Oestergaard","orcid":"0000-0001-5757-1806","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Per","key":"kallestruppau","last":"Kallestrup","locid":"edc492e6-b4ed-4c50-8948-986e774b6f70","name":"Kallestrup, Per","orcid":"0000-0001-6041-4510","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jemma","key":"hawkinspau","last":"Hawkins","name":"Hawkins, Jemma","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Knud","key":"ryompau","last":"Ryom","locid":"6294b7a6-a4e0-418b-9d18-5d000ab81d14","name":"Ryom, Knud","orcid":"0000-0001-5947-3038","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35656623"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/14034948221098929","issn":["14034948","16511905"],"issn_org":["14034956"],"issue":"8","journal":"Scandinavian Journal of Public Health","pages":"1258-1265","publisher":"Taylor and Francis Ltd.","status":"p","title":"Scandinavian Journal of Public Health","volume":"51"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85131574219"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-27795468-8ef6-4b81-9acf-773669aa8ce8"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Study design of 'Move More' : Development and feasibility of a social-prescribing intervention to increase physical activity among inactive Danes","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-151992","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Iron metabolism and markers hereof are altered in anorexia nervosa (AN) but far from completely understood. We report a case of extreme hyperferritinemia in a patient with AN and discuss the possible mechanisms and current knowledge about the association between hyperferritinemia and AN. A 20-year-old woman with a history of AN presented with bradycardia, weariness, and malaise in addition to an incidentally very high ferritin level. The symptoms disappeared spontaneously after a short admission. There were no signs suggestive of systemic, hematological, or malignant disease causing the very high concentration of ferritin. Her body weight was in decline, leading up to admission, but did initially increase after discharge accompanied by declining ferritin concentration. However, a clear association between ferritin dynamics and weight changes or physical activity was not identified and neither were other causes of the hyperferritinemia. Around one in four patients with AN have increased ferritin concentrations. Our case represents the highest ferritin concentration reported in a patient with AN without other underlying causes or comorbidities.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Amanoal, Christine Hvolby","Kongsgaard Nielsen, Lene","Thomsen, Henrik Holm"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-258778"],"filters":{"added":"2023-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"extreme hyperferritinemia in restrictive type anorexia nervosa","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1530/EDM-22-0405","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-258778","identifier":[{"id":"PMC10083646","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"36951802","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85152718652","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-e2cc65e8-5d84-4f43-b05e-5e5b90f705cf"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9A02278480641D21A43242E540F62917"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1530/EDM-22-0405","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Regional Hospital of Viborg"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Medicinsk afdeling, Viborg"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christine Hvolby","key":"amanoalpau","last":"Amanoal","name":"Amanoal, Christine Hvolby","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lene Kongsgaard","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"897c29d3-45a2-4178-81b1-b0ba61db5a25","name":"Kongsgaard Nielsen, Lene","name_org":"Nielsen, Lene Kongsgaard","orcid":"0000-0003-2083-3870","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Holm","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"8a62ba63-8721-464e-b09f-c97db8bb4ad9","name":"Thomsen, Henrik Holm","orcid":"0000-0003-0522-0320","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36951802"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1530/edm-22-0405","issn":["20520573"],"issue":"1","journal":"Endocrinology, Diabetes & Metabolism Case Reports","publisher":"BioScientifica Ltd.","status":"p","title":"Endocrinology, Diabetes & Metabolism Case Reports","volume":"2023"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85152718652"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-e2cc65e8-5d84-4f43-b05e-5e5b90f705cf"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Extreme hyperferritinemia in restrictive type anorexia nervosa","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-258778","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"A link between influenza infection and cardiovascular morbidity has been known for almost a century. This narrative review examined the cardiovascular complications associated with influenza and the potential mechanisms behind this relationship. The most common reported cardiovascular complications are cardiovascular death, myocardial infarction, and heart failure hospitalization. There are multiple proposed mechanisms driving the increased risk of cardiovascular complications. These mechanics involve influenza-specific effects such as direct cardiac infection and endothelial dysfunction leading to plaque destabilization and rupture, but also hypoxaemia and systemic inflammatory responses including increased metabolic demand, biomechanical stress, and hypercoagulability. The significance of the individual effects is unclear, and thus whether influenza directly or indirectly causes cardiovascular events is unknown. In conclusion, the risk of acute cardiovascular morbidity and mortality is elevated during influenza infection. The proposed underlying pathophysiological mechanisms support this association, but systemic responses to infection may drive this relationship.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Skaarup, Kristoffer Grundtvig","Modin, Daniel","Nielsen, Lene","Jensen, Jens Ulrik Stæhr","Biering-Sørensen, Tor"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-439826"],"filters":{"added":"2023-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Activation","Acute myocardial infarction","Cardiovascular disease","Chlamydia pneumoniae","Endothelial Cells","Excess mortality","Expression","Heart failure","Inflammatory cytokines","Influenza Infection","Myocardial Infarction","Pathophysiology","Streptococcus pneumoniae","atherosclerotic plaques","virus infection"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"