au"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kristine Sloth","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","name":"Thomsen, Kristine Sloth","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lydia Marie Brosbøl","key":"fredensborgpau","last":"Fredensborg","name":"Fredensborg, Lydia Marie Brosbøl","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte Gad","key":"tousigpau","last":"Tousig","name":"Tousig, Charlotte Gad","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Tanja","key":"christiansenpau","last":"Christiansen","name":"Christiansen, Tanja","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["16020162"],"issn_org":["22462155"],"issue":"4","journal":"Uddannelsesnyt","pages":"10-14","publisher":"Fagligt Selskab for Undervisende Sygeplejersker","status":"p","title":"Uddannelsesnyt","volume":"33"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","UCL University College","University Colleges"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ucl-07af1ad1-f912-46ea-943d-3aeecc8b1939"],"src":["UCL University College"],"title":"Tværprofessionelt simulationsscenarie i klinikken","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-680886","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Background The purpose of this study was to assess whether influenza vaccination has an impact on the risk of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19). Methods A cohort of 46 112 healthcare workers were tested for antibodies against severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) and filled in a survey on COVID-19 symptoms, hospitalization, and influenza vaccination. Results The risk ratio of hospitalization due to SARS-CoV-2 for influenza vaccinated compared with unvaccinated participants was 1.00 for the seasonal vaccination in 2019/2020 (confidence interval, .56-1.78, P = 1.00). Likewise, no clinical effect of influenza vaccination on development of antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 was found. Conclusions The present findings indicate that influenza vaccination does not affect the risk of SARS-CoV-2 infection or COVID-19. This cohort study of 46 112 healthcare workers examined the effect of influenza vaccination on hospitalization and symptoms due to COVID-19 and development of antibodies against SARS-CoV-2. Influenza vaccination had no effect on the specified outcomes.","author_count":33,"contributors":["Kristensen, Jonas Henrik","Hasselbalch, Rasmus Bo","Pries-Heje, Mia","Nielsen, Pernille Brok","Knudsen, Andreas D.","Fogh, Kamille","Norsk, Jakob Boesgaard","Eiken, Aleksander","Andersen, Ove","Fischer, Thea K.","Jensen, Claus Antonio Juul","Torp-Pedersen, Christian","Rungby, Jørgen","Ditlev, Sisse Bolm","Hageman, Ida","Møgelvang, Rasmus","Gybel-Brask, Mikkel","Dessau, Ram Benny","Sørensen, Erik","Harritshøj, Lene","Folke, Fredrik","Moller, Maria Elizabeth Engel","Benfield, Thomas","Ullum, Henrik","Jorgensen, Charlotte Svaerke","Ostrowski, Sisse Rye","Nielsen, Susanne Dam","Bundgaard, Henning","Iversen, Kasper"],"cuid":["S02-094.93-039725"],"filters":{"added":"2022-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Kristensen, Jonas Henrik"],"keyword":["COVID 19 prevention 