Neurology","paperid":"233","status":"p","title":"BMC Neurology","volume":"22"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85132892857"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-0048aed7-50fa-4759-a5c5-6c41e1ce1b77"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm and cerebral toxoplasmosis : a case report","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-393642","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Background Preeclampsia is associated with increased risk of cardiovascular disease later in life, but studies suggest that women with previous preeclampsia are not aware of this. Little is known about how these women perceive the condition and the associated long-term risks. We examined the experiences and perceptions of preeclampsia and the increased risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) later in life among Danish women with previous preeclampsia and their attitudes towards CVD risk screening. Methods Ten individual semi-structured interviews were conducted with women with previous preeclampsia. Data were analysed using thematic analysis. Results We identified six themes: 1) Experiences and perceptions of being diagnosed with preeclampsia, 2) Awareness about increased risk of CVD later in life, 3) Knowledge as a precondition for action, 4) The perception of CVD risk as being modifiable, 5) Motivators for and barriers to a healthy lifestyle, and 6) Screening for CVD. Awareness of the severity of preeclampsia was limited prior to being diagnosed. Particularly among those with few or no symptoms, preeclampsia was perceived as a non-severe condition, which was further reinforced by the experience of having received very little information. Nonetheless, some women were shocked by the diagnosis and feared for the health of the offspring. Many women also experienced physical and psychological consequences of preeclampsia. Awareness of the increased risk of later CVD was lacking; yet, when informed, the women considered this to be essential knowledge to be able to act accordingly. The risk of future CVD was perceived to be partly modifiable with a healthy lifestyle, and the women expressed a need for counselling on appropriate lifestyle changes to reduce CVD risk. Other factors were also mentioned as imperative for lifestyle changes, including social support. The women were generally positive towards potential future screening for CVD because it could provide them with information about their health condition. Conclusions After preeclampsia, women experienced a lack of knowledge on preeclampsia and the increased risk of CVD later in life. Improved information and follow-up after preeclampsia, including guidance on CVD risk reduction and support from health professionals and family, are warranted.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Nielsen, Lene Musfelt","Hauge, Maria Guldbrandt","Ersboll, Anne S.","Johansen, Marianne","Linde, Jesper James","Damm, Peter","Nielsen, Karoline Kragelund"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-450193"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cardiovascular disease","Disease","Preeclampsia","Qualitative Research"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"women s perceptions of cardiovascular risk after preeclampsia a qualitative study","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12884-022-05179-9","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-450193","identifier":[{"id":"000881984600001","type":"wos"},{"id":"PMC9650891","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85141668284","type":"scopus"},{"id":"36368968","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-5ed232ab-b066-40af-a05c-903965fa2e21"],"keyword":["Cardiovascular disease","DISEASE","Preeclampsia","Qualitative research"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E3659813EB71C3A23D2DE24C1B37E0FA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12884-022-05179-9","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/326342771/Fulltext.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Department of Public Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Lene Musfelt","key":"nielsenlenemusfeltpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f4719bef-6a6f-4863-823f-4c76c61e676e","name":"Nielsen, Lene Musfelt","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Maria Guldbrandt","key":"haugemariaguldbrandtpau","last":"Hauge","name":"Hauge, Maria Guldbrandt","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anne S.","key":"ersbollannespau","last":"Ersboll","name":"Ersboll, Anne S.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Marianne","key":"johansenmariannepau","last":"Johansen","name":"Johansen, Marianne","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jesper James","key":"lindejesperjamespau","last":"Linde","name":"Linde, Jesper James","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Peter","key":"dammpeterpau","last":"Damm","locid":"884d03d5-5981-4d33-80c6-adb21f836deb","name":"Damm, Peter","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Karoline Kragelund","key":"nielsenkarolinekragelundpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Karoline Kragelund","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36368968"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12884-022-05179-9","issn":["14712393"],"issue":"1","journal":"BMC Pregnancy and Childbirth","paperid":"832","status":"p","title":"BMC Pregnancy and Childbirth","volume":"22"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85141668284"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-5ed232ab-b066-40af-a05c-903965fa2e21"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Women's perceptions of cardiovascular risk after preeclampsia : a qualitative study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-450193","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The repeated, rapid and often pronounced patterns of evolutionary divergence observed in insular plants, or the ‘plant island syndrome’, include changes in leaf phenotypes, growth, as well as the acquisition of a perennial lifestyle. Here, we sequence and describe the genome of the critically endangered, Galápagos-endemic species Scalesia atractyloides Arnot., obtaining a chromosome-resolved, 3.2-Gbp assembly containing 43,093 candidate gene models. Using a combination of fossil transposable elements, k-mer spectra analyses and orthologue assignment, we identify the two ancestral genomes, and date their divergence and the polyploidization event, concluding that the ancestor of all extant Scalesia species was an allotetraploid. There are a comparable number of genes and transposable elements across the two subgenomes, and while their synteny has been mostly conserved, we find multiple inversions that may have facilitated adaptation. We identify clear signatures of selection across genes associated with vascular development, growth, adaptation to salinity and flowering time, thus finding compelling evidence for a genomic basis of the island syndrome in one of Darwin’s giant daisies.","author_count":29,"contributors":["Cerca, José","Petersen, Bent","Lazaro-Guevara, José Miguel","Rivera-Colón, Angel","Birkeland, Siri","Vizueta, Joel","Li, Siyu","Li, Qionghou","Loureiro, João","Kosawang, Chatchai","Díaz, Patricia Jaramillo","Rivas-Torres, Gonzalo","Fernández-Mazuecos, Mario","Vargas, Pablo","McCauley, Ross A.","Petersen, Gitte","Santos-Bay, Luisa","Wales, Nathan","Catchen, Julian M.","Machado, Daniel","Nowak, Michael D.","Suh, Alexander","Sinha, Neelima R.","Nielsen, Lene Rostgaard","Seberg, Ole","Gilbert, M. Thomas P.","Leebens-Mack, James H.","Rieseberg, Loren H.","Martin, Michael D."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-525090"],"filters":{"added":"2022-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","America, South","Asia, South","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Canada","Ecuador","Malaysia","Norway","Portugal","Spain","Sweden","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Asian Institute of Medicine, Science and Technology","Charles Darwin Foundation Santa Cruz","Fort Lewis College","Norwegian University of Life Sciences","Norwegian University of Science and Technology","Stockholm University","The Spanish National Research Council (CSIC)","Universidad Autónoma de Madrid","Universidad San Francisco de Quito","University of British Columbia","University of California at Davis","University of Coimbra","University of East Anglia","University of Florida","University of Georgia","University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign","University of Málaga","University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill","University of Oslo","University of York","Uppsala University"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the genomic basis of the plant island syndrome in darwin s giant daisies","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-31280-w","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-525090","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9240058","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85132977043","type":"scopus"},{"id":"35764640","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-dc65f041-f8b7-4375-b1bb-2fc5fdd01abc"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-26EF8CB4AAA602A40B4802D036544261"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-31280-w","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/315856484/s41467_022_31280_w.pdf","version":"Published 