2d-4279-987d-13f8250ee1d0","sdu-ff739ff3-689e-4f0e-979f-20aeb1feca26"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Can missing PRO data be handled appropriately by last observation carried forward (LOCF)?","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S05-088.71-012128","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Paludiculture, and in particular the cultivation of perennial grasses as biomass feedstock for green biorefineries, may be aneconomic and environmentally sustainable option for agricultural peatlands in temperate regions. However, the optimal biomassquality for protein extraction from flood-tolerant grasses is largely unknown. The aim of this study was to define the combinedeffect of harvest and fertilization frequency, with one to five annual cuts, on protein yield and extractability for the grasses tallfescue (TF) and reed canary grass (RCG), cultivated on an agricultural fen peatland in Denmark.The content of protein fractions was determined according to the Cornell Net Carbohydrate and Protein System (CNCPS). Weassessed protein extractability by lab-scale biorefinery techniques using a screw-press followed by acid precipitation of trueprotein. The two methods were compared to correlate potential extractable protein yields with actual biorefinery outputs. Wefound the highest annual biomass and crude protein (CP) yields in the two cut treatments, with 13.4 and 15.6 t dry matter (DM)ha-1 yr-1, containing 2.9 - 3.4 t CP ha-1 yr-1 for TF and RCG, respectively. The highest neutral-extractable (fractions B1 and B2)true protein yields of 1.1 and 1.5 t ha-1 yr-1 were found in the two cut treatments, representing 39 % (TF) - 45 % (RCG) of total CP.Using biorefining techniques, we were able to precipitate up to 2.2 t DM ha-1 yr-1 of protein concentrate, containing up to 39 %CP. Significant correlations between methods were found, with a distinct relationship between CNCPS fractions B1 + B2 and CPyield of the protein concentrate, indicating the suitability of the CNCPS as an indicator for extractable protein yields.Biomass and CP yields were not significantly improved beyond two annual cuts. However, timing and harvest frequenciessignificantly affected plant maturity and consequently extractable CP contents and protein concentrate yields. We conclude that TFand RCG are promising feedstocks for green biorefineries due to high biomass, extractable CP, and protein concentrate yields, andhighlight the potential of flood-tolerant grasses, cultivated on wet agricultural peatlands, for an enhanced valorisation beyond thecommon utilisation for bioenergy","author_count":4,"contributors":["Nielsen, Claudia","Stødkilde-Jørgensen, Lene","Jørgensen, Uffe","Lærke, Poul Erik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-269846"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Claudia"],"keyword":["Biorefinery","Crude protein","Paludiculture","Plant fractionation","Reed canary grass","Tall Fescue"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"effects of harvest and fertilization frequency on protein yield and extractability from flood tolerant perennial grasses cultivated on a fen peatland","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fenvs.2021.619258","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-269846","identifier":[{"id":"85105384528","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-f65417ec-6b5c-4dfd-a684-3bbe598bcdf6"],"keyword":["Biorefinery","crude protein","paludiculture","plant fractionation","reed canary grass","tall fescue"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AA52E9DA39F18A6EAC7B03E3740EA42D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fenvs.2021.619258","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, CBIO - Centre for Circular Bioeconomy, Foulum","Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Agroecology, Department of Agroecology - Climate and Water"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claudia","key":"kallanielsenpau","last":"Kalla Nielsen","locid":"66d68122-06cf-4e2c-9f01-b6df8fdecb1c","name":"Nielsen, Claudia","name_org":"Kalla Nielsen, Claudia","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lene","key":"stødkildepau","last":"Stødkilde","locid":"7edb699b-a75e-48cd-a351-9ee9e1321d4d","name":"Stødkilde-Jørgensen, Lene","name_org":"Stødkilde, Lene","orcid":"0000-0001-9434-0118","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Uffe","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"efa7e924-c197-4060-8904-110cf0a30837","name":"Jørgensen, Uffe","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Poul Erik","key":"lærkepau","last":"Lærke","locid":"03fcd9e2-3402-466b-86fc-f3c8163d9306","name":"Lærke, Poul Erik","orcid":"0000-0002-2224-3893","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fenvs.2021.619258","issn":["2296665X"],"journal":"Frontiers in Environmental Science","paperid":"619258","publisher":"Frontiers Media SA","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Environmental Science","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85105384528"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-f65417ec-6b5c-4dfd-a684-3bbe598bcdf6"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Effects of harvest and fertilization frequency on protein yield and extractability from flood-tolerant perennial grasses cultivated on a fen peatland","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-269846","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background Transcutaneous auricular vagus nerve stimulation (ta-VNS) is a new non-invasive technique developed as treatment option for drug resistant epilepsy. A few studies have been carried out showing that the efficacy and tolerability of ta-VNS is comparable with traditional implanted VNS but the feasibility of the therapy has been poorly described. This study aimed to explore potential clinical benefits of ta-VNS and to evaluate adaptation, compliance, as well as the usability of the device from a service design perspective. Methods A prospective, multicenter, clinical, investigator-initiated trial was conducted using the NEMOS® ta-VNS device. After eight weeks baseline, all subjects started ta-VNS with individually adjusted currents for four hours per day for six-months (first endpoint) followed by optional 12 months follow-up (second endpoint). The primary outcome was six months retention rate of ta-VNS therapy. Secondary outcomes included the user retention rate at 12 months follow-up, compliance, changes in scores of psychometric measures. For the study of feasibility, a service design questionnaire on medical devices used in the home was developed. Results In total 37 subjects had been included in the study after 45 months where the study was prematurely terminated due to recruitment problems and due to a high drop-out rate. Twenty-two subjects (59 %) completed the first six months of the study and in total six subjects (16 %) completed the following 12 months follow-up. The reasons for discontinuation were a mixture of medical and practical issues of which the majority were related to a combination of both. Those, who managed to continue to use ta-VNS throughout the study, gave generally higher scores for the device usability and compatibility with lifestyle. The study turned out to be inadequately powered to reach any conclusion in terms of the clinical benefits of ta-VNS but present an example of difficulties that are encountered in conducting high-quality studies with digital devices. Conclusion The feasibility of ta-VNS therapy showed to be relatively modest which is most likely due to practical usability issues and lifestyle fits. The results of this study stress the importance of generating data based on patients experiences at an early stage during the development phase and when designing clinical trials on medical devices that depend on patient’s active participation and motivation.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Sabers, Anne","Wagner, Sophia Aumüller","Christensen, Lars Rune","Henning, Oliver","Kostov, Konstantin","Lossius, Morten","Majoie, Marian","Mertens, Ann","Nielsen, Lene","Vonck, Kristl","Wagner, Louis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383493"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","Netherlands","Norway"],"glorg":["4Brain, Department of Neurology, Ghent University Hospital","Academic Center for Epileptology Kempenhaeghe and Maastricht University Medical Center, Heeze and Maastricht","All Global Affiliations","The National Center for Epilepsy, Rikshospitalet, University Hospital, Sandvika, Norway"],"keyword":["Clinical trial design","Drug resistant epilepsy","Human Computer Interaction","Neuromodulation","Non invasive auricular vagus nerve stimulation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"feasibility of transcutaneous auricular vagus nerve stimulation in treatment of drug resistant epilepsy a multicenter prospective study","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86429887/ta_VNS_til_upload.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383493","identifier":[{"id":"85115963885","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-978d8e24-1631-4f73-bb04-c78c88cdb8ff"],"keyword":["Clinical trial design","Drug resistant epilepsy","Human computer interaction","Neuromodulation","Non-invasive auricular vagus nerve stimulation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B6D168AAA3D146D65510CB4A54680AD8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86429887/ta_VNS_til_upload.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"no","key":"the national center for epilepsy rikshospitalet university hospital sandvika norway","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"The National Center for Epilepsy, Rikshospitalet, University Hospital, Sandvika, Norway","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"nl","key":"academic center for epileptology kempenhaeghe and maastricht university medical center heeze and maastricht","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Academic Center for Epileptology Kempenhaeghe and Maastricht University Medical Center, Heeze and Maastricht","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"be","key":"4brain department of neurology ghent university hospital","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"4Brain, Department of Neurology, Ghent University Hospital","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne","key":"sabersannepau","last":"Sabers","name":"Sabers, Anne","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sophia Aumüller","key":"wagnersophiaaumüllerpau","last":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Sophia Aumüller","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars Rune","key":"christensenlarsrunepau","last":"Christensen","locid":"92f6c3f3-0ad2-40fc-b278-69170f87567d","name":"Christensen, Lars Rune","orcid":"0000-0001-7204-465X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"henningoliverpau","last":"Henning","name":"Henning, Oliver","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantin","key":"kostovkonstantinpau","last":"Kostov","name":"Kostov, Konstantin","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten","key":"lossiusmortenpau","last":"Lossius","name":"Lossius, Morten","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Marian","key":"majoiemarianpau","last":"Majoie","name":"Majoie, Marian","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Ann","key":"mertensannpau","last":"Mertens","name":"Mertens, Ann","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"nielsenlenepau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"1052b289-f510-4c5a-9662-2db48c12ebc2","name":"Nielsen, Lene","orcid":"0000-0003-1768-7147","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Kristl","key":"vonckkristlpau","last":"Vonck","name":"Vonck, Kristl","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Louis","key":"wagnerlouispau","last":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Louis","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.eplepsyres.2021.106776","issn":["09201211","09229833"],"issn_org":["09201211"],"journal":"Epilepsy Research","pages":"106776","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Epilepsy Research","volume":"177"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85115963885"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-978d8e24-1631-4f73-bb04-c78c88cdb8ff"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Feasibility of transcutaneous auricular vagus nerve stimulation in treatment of drug resistant epilepsy : A multicenter prospective study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383493","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Glibenclamide inhibits sulfonylurea receptor (SUR), which regulates several ion channels including SUR1-transient receptor potential melastatin 4 (SUR1-TRPM4) channel and ATP-sensitive potassium (KATP) channel. Stroke upregulates SURl-TRPM4 channel, which causes a rapid edema formation and brain swelling. Glibenclamide may antagonize the formation of cerebral edema during stroke. Preclinical studies showed that glibenclamide inhibits KATP channel-induced vasodilation without altering the basal vascular tone. The in vivo human cerebrovascular effects of glibenclamide have not previously been investigated. In a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, three-way cross-over study, we used advanced 3 T MRI methods to investigate the effects of glibenclamide and KATP channel opener levcromakalim on mean global cerebral blood flow (CBF) and intra- and extracranial artery circumferences in 15 healthy volunteers. Glibenclamide administration did not alter the mean global CBF and the basal vascular tone. Following levcromakalim infusion, we observed a 14% increase of the mean global CBF and an 8% increase of middle cerebral artery (MCA) circumference, and glibenclamide did not attenuate levcromakalim-induced vascular changes. Collectively, the findings demonstrate the vital role of KATP channels in cerebrovascular hemodynamic and indicate that glibenclamide does not inhibit the protective effects of KATP channel activation during hypoxia and ischemia-induced brain injury.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Al-Karagholi, Mohammad Al Mahdi","Ghanizada, Hashmat","Nielsen, Cherie Amalie Waldorff","Ansari, Assan","Gram, Christian","Younis, Samaria","Vestergaard, Mark B.","Larsson, Henrik B W","Skovgaard, Lene Theil","Amin, Faisal Mohammad","Ashina, Messoud"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-428749"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Human models","KATP channel","Levcromakalim","Migraine","Stroke"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cerebrovascular effects of glibenclamide investigated using high resolution magnetic resonance imaging in healthy volunteers","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-428749","identifier":[{"id":"85092282639","type":"scopus"},{"id":"33028147","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8142144","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-3b32b4e8-bdd0-457f-a57e-1189d4b45860"],"keyword":["Human models","KATP channel","levcromakalim","migraine","stroke"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-81E6C92B86D569B1FBAA906354874794"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Mohammad Al Mahdi","key":"alkaragholipau","last":"Al-Karagholi","locid":"5c140544-fa4a-47f0-9d6f-d565124b42db","name":"Al-Karagholi, Mohammad Al Mahdi","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Hashmat","key":"ghanizadapau","last":"Ghanizada","name":"Ghanizada, Hashmat","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Cherie Amalie Waldorff","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Cherie Amalie Waldorff","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Assan","key":"ansaripau","last":"Ansari","name":"Ansari, Assan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Christian","key":"grampau","last":"Gram","name":"Gram, Christian","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Samaria","key":"younispau","last":"Younis","name":"Younis, Samaria","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Mark B.","key":"vestergaardpau","last":"Vestergaard","name":"Vestergaard, Mark B.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik B.W.","key":"larssonpau","last":"Larsson","locid":"3c09e175-dfd1-4df5-9132-536a42c19373","name":"Larsson, Henrik B W","name_org":"Larsson, Henrik B.W.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lene Theil","key":"skovgaardpau","last":"Skovgaard","locid":"10dc01f7-6427-497e-bad6-fb5f5b3c6a70","name":"Skovgaard, Lene Theil","orcid":"0000-0002-4708-4948","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Faisal Mohammad","key":"aminpau","last":"Amin","locid":"5cea7989-5872-4bc7-bc2d-220a5536f5fb","name":"Amin, Faisal Mohammad","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Messoud","key":"ashinapau","last":"Ashina","locid":"0e941844-26c5-4777-a66a-64a163c19cd2","name":"Ashina, Messoud","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33028147"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/0271678x20959294","issn":["0271678X","15597016"],"issn_org":["0271678X"],"issue":"6","journal":"Journal of Cerebral Blood Flow and Metabolism","pages":"1328-1337","status":"p","title":"Journal of Cerebral Blood Flow and Metabolism","volume":"41"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85092282639"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-3b32b4e8-bdd0-457f-a57e-1189d4b45860"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Cerebrovascular effects of glibenclamide investigated using high-resolution magnetic resonance imaging in healthy volunteers","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-428749","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Aims/hypothesis: We aimed to identify potentially modifiable risk factors and causes for preterm delivery in women with type 1 or type 2 (pre-existing) diabetes. Methods: A secondary analysis of a prospective cohort study of 203 women with pre-existing diabetes (117 type 1 and 86 type 2 diabetes) was performed. Consecutive singleton pregnancies were included at the first antenatal visit between September 2015 and February 2018. Results: In total, 27% (n = 55) of the 203 women delivered preterm at median 36 + 0 weeks. When stratified by diabetes type, 33% of women with type 1 diabetes delivered preterm compared with 20% in women with type 2 diabetes (p = 0.04). Women delivering preterm were characterised by a higher prevalence of pre-existing kidney involvement (microalbuminuria or diabetic nephropathy) (16% vs 3%, p = 0.002), preeclampsia (26% vs 5%, p < 0.001), higher positive ultrasound estimated fetal weight deviation at 27 gestational weeks (2.7% vs −1.6% from the mean, p = 0.008), higher gestational weight gain (399 g/week vs 329 g/week, p = 0.01) and similar HbA1c levels in early pregnancy (51 mmol/mol [6.8%] vs 49 [6.6%], p = 0.22) when compared with women delivering at term. Independent risk factors for preterm delivery were pre-existing kidney involvement (OR 12.71 [95% CI 3.0, 53.79]), higher gestational weight gain (per 100 g/week, OR 1.25 [1.02, 1.54]), higher positive ultrasound estimated fetal weight deviation at 27 gestational weeks (% from the mean, OR 1.07 [1.03, 1.12]) and preeclampsia (OR 7.04 [2.34, 21.19]). Two-thirds of preterm deliveries were indicated and one-third were spontaneous. Several contributing factors to indicated preterm delivery were often present in each woman. The main indications were suspected fetal asphyxia (45%), hypertensive disorders (34%), fetal overgrowth (13%) and maternal indications (8%). Suspected fetal asphyxia mainly included falling insulin requirement and abnormal fetal haemodynamics. Conclusions/interpretations: Presence of preeclampsia, higher positive ultrasound estimated fetal weight deviation at 27 gestational weeks and higher gestational weight gain were independent potentially modifiable risk factors for preterm delivery in this cohort of women with pre-existing diabetes. Indicated preterm delivery was common with suspected fetal asphyxia or preeclampsia as the most prevalent causes. Prospective studies evaluating whether modifying these predictors will reduce the prevalence of preterm delivery are warranted. Graphical abstract: [Figure not available: see fulltext.].","author_count":10,"contributors":["Søholm, Julie C.","Vestgaard, Marianne","Ásbjörnsdóttir, Björg","Do, Nicoline Callesen","Pedersen, Berit Woetmann","Storgaard, Lone","Nielsen, Birgitte Bruun","Ringholm, Lene","Damm, Peter","Mathiesen, Elisabeth R."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-485977"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Blood Pressure","Diabetes","Fetal overgrowth","Gestational weight gain","Glycaemic control","Hypertension","Preeclampsia","Pregnancy Outcome","Preterm delivery"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"potentially modifiable risk factors of preterm delivery in women with type 000000000001 and type 000000000002 diabetes","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-485977","identifier":[{"id":"85108322801","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34146144","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-9acbd61f-c634-4260-b4ab-ddce18a5279f"],"keyword":["Blood pressure","Diabetes","Fetal overgrowth","Gestational weight gain","Glycaemic control","Hypertension","Preeclampsia","Pregnancy outcome","Preterm delivery"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4DB44AED62010F5399E4297DB023183D"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Julie C.","key":"søholmpau","last":"Søholm","name":"Søholm, Julie C.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Marianne","key":"vestgaardpau","last":"Vestgaard","name":"Vestgaard, Marianne","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","first":"Björg","key":"ásbjörnsdóttirpau","last":"Ásbjörnsdóttir","locid":"bf1d455a-519b-4a24-a4f1-daf63c80355a","name":"Ásbjörnsdóttir, Björg","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Nicoline C.","key":"dopau","last":"Do","locid":"76e50523-e4aa-4793-88c9-7ab796eb1a9c","name":"Do, Nicoline Callesen","name_org":"Do, Nicoline C.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Berit W.","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"687d0aad-7fb2-4546-94c1-2cb36e3c255c","name":"Pedersen, Berit Woetmann","name_org":"Pedersen, Berit W.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lone","key":"storgaardpau","last":"Storgaard","name":"Storgaard, Lone","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Birgitte B.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"d7bd20c0-28c4-4ada-9a91-f87