{"abstract":"Practitioners in user-centric industries have increasingly recognized the applicability of personas. However, the methods used to create personas in different domains remain inconsistent and unsystematic. We analyzed 51 studies focused on designing personas for professional purposes and found the practice most prevalent in the user experience design, healthcare, market research, and social media strategy domains. Within these domains, user experience design personas are characterized by their focus on user activity goals, health personas on medical patients’ physical symptoms, market research personas on customers’ lifestyles, and social media strategy personas on interactions within and between online communities. We identify and compare the elements in the personas. Based on these, we provide guidelines for professionals interested in developing personas for understanding barriers to positive user experience, recruiting users, and building online communities, including how to represent persona details related to lifestyle and health, contexts of product usage, and scaling of online data.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Guan, Kathleen","Salminen, joni","Nielsen, Lene","Jung, Soon-Gyo","Jansen, Bernard J."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382981"],"event":{"acronym":"HICSS","title":"Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"country":["Qatar","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Georgetown University","Qatar Computing Research institute"],"keyword":["personas professionals use"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"information design for personas in four professional domains of user experience design healthcare market research and social media strategy","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.24251/HICSS.2021.540","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382981","identifier":[{"id":"85108326905","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-74036bf4-98b5-48b7-8e0c-7f7566cb4546"],"keyword":["personas, professionals, use"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-A0DCE4E9247B07A4E88DE7C0ADFEC414"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85929362/HICSS_paper_Persona_Domains_v4.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.24251/HICSS.2021.540","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"http://hdl.handle.net/10125/71157","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"georgetown university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Georgetown University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"qa","key":"qatar computing research institute","loc":["Qatar","Asia, Middle East"],"name":"Qatar Computing Research institute","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kathleen","key":"guanpau","last":"Guan","name":"Guan, Kathleen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"joni","key":"salminenpau","last":"Salminen","name":"Salminen, joni","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"1052b289-f510-4c5a-9662-2db48c12ebc2","name":"Nielsen, Lene","orcid":"0000-0003-1768-7147","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Soon-Gyo","key":"jungpau","last":"Jung","name":"Jung, Soon-Gyo","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Bernard J.","key":"jansenpau","last":"Jansen","name":"Jansen, Bernard J.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.24251/hicss.2021.540","eisbn":["9780998133140"],"pages":"4446-4455","publisher":"University of Hawai'i at Manoa","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 54th Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-74036bf4-98b5-48b7-8e0c-7f7566cb4546"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Information Design for Personas in Four Professional Domains of User Experience Design, Healthcare, Market Research, and Social Media Strategy","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382981","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"author_count":2,"contributors":["Schøsler, Lene","Nielsen, Marita Akhøj"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-463189"],"filters":{"added":"2022-04","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"leonora christinas franske selvbiografi diplomatarisk udgave og dansk oversættelse","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-463189","ids":["ku-74a4c33b-03d1-4f1b-b1a6-f855cec64c93"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B07E64E7ABBA60A52304222F4CF2AB70"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of English, Germanic and Romance Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Lene","key":"schøslerped","last":"Schøsler","locid":"4638bb59-4175-4e41-9a72-07d80d794ed1","name":"Schøsler, Lene","rank":1,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"1","first":"Marita Akhøj","key":"nielsenped","last":"Nielsen","locid":"dd03e13d-42ef-4a97-aa18-6967bd5d3569","name":"Nielsen, Marita Akhøj","rank":2,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788775330577"],"pages":"315","place":"København","publisher":"Det Danske Sprog- og Litteraturselskab","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-74a4c33b-03d1-4f1b-b1a6-f855cec64c93"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Leonora Christinas franske selvbiografi. : Diplomatarisk udgave og dansk oversættelse","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-463189","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Context: Disorders affecting adrenal steroidogenesis promote an imbalance in the normally tightly controlled secretion of mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids, and androgens. This may lead to differences/disorders of sex development in the fetus, as seen in virilized girls with congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH). Despite the important endocrine function of human fetal adrenals, neither normal nor dysregulated adrenal steroidogenesis is understood in detail. Objective: Due to significant differences in adrenal steroidogenesis between human and model species (except higher primates), we aimed to establish a human fetal adrenal model that enables examination of both de novo and manipulated adrenal steroidogenesis. Design and Setting: Human adrenal tissue from 54 1st trimester fetuses were cultured ex vivo as intact tissue fragments for 7 or 14 days. Main Outcome Measures: Model validation included examination of postculture tissue morphology, viability, apoptosis, and quantification of steroid hormones secreted to the culture media measured by liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry. Results: The culture approach maintained cell viability, preserved cell populations of all fetal adrenal zones, and recapitulated de novo adrenal steroidogenesis based on continued secretion of steroidogenic intermediates, glucocorticoids, and androgens. Adrenocorticotropic hormone and ketoconazole treatment of ex vivo cultured human fetal adrenal tissue resulted in the stimulation of steroidogenesis and inhibition of androgen secretion, respectively, demonstrating a treatment-specific response. Conclusions: Together, these data indicate that ex vivo culture of human fetal adrenal tissue constitutes a novel approach to investigate local effects of pharmaceutical exposures or emerging therapeutic options targeting imbalanced steroidogenesis in adrenal disorders, including CAH.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Melau, Cecilie","Nielsen, John E.","Perlman, Signe","Lundvall, Lene","Langhoff Thuesen, Lea","Juul Hare, Kristine","Schou Hammerum, Mette","Frederiksen, Hanne","Mitchell, Rod T.","Juul, Anders","Jørgensen, Anne Juul"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-473792"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Edinburgh"],"keyword":["ACTH","Endocrine activity","Ex vivo culture","Ketoconazole","Steroidogenesis"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"establishment of a novel human fetal adrenal culture model that supports de novo and manipulated steroidogenesis","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/334433579/483869725_2_34.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-473792","identifier":[{"id":"33212489","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85102910589","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-8658a448-6b64-4c00-a127-6518152fb667"],"keyword":["ACTH","androgen biosynthesis","endocrine activity","ex vivo culture","ketoconazole","steroidogenesis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-87529F567BBF3C6582462DF0DDB8B390"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/334433579/483869725_2_34.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Bispebjerg and Frederiksberg"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of edinburgh","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Edinburgh","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Cecilie","key":"melaupau","last":"Melau","name":"Melau, Cecilie","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"John E.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, John E.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Signe","key":"perlmanpau","last":"Perlman","name":"Perlman, Signe","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lene","key":"lundvallpau","last":"Lundvall","name":"Lundvall, Lene","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lea","key":"langhoffthuesenpau","last":"Langhoff Thuesen","name":"Langhoff Thuesen, Lea","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Kristine","key":"juulharepau","last":"Juul Hare","locid":"6c001dde-dccd-46ac-96f6-3fe10ec9e201","name":"Juul Hare, Kristine","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Mette","key":"schouhammerumpau","last":"Schou Hammerum","name":"Schou Hammerum, Mette","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Hanne","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","name":"Frederiksen, Hanne","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Rod T.","key":"mitchellpau","last":"Mitchell","name":"Mitchell, Rod T.","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anders","key":"juulpau","last":"Juul","locid":"f9b11983-c7c8-4111-b119-4de471572898","name":"Juul, Anders","orcid":"0000-0002-0534-4350","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anne","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"8ad1fa92-d0aa-40ab-ac26-47b7a37bb6e8","name":"Jørgensen, Anne Juul","name_org":"Jørgensen, Anne","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33212489"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1210/clinem/dgaa852","issn":["0021972X","19457197"],"issn_org":["0021972X"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism","pages":"843-857","status":"p","title":"Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism","volume":"106"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85102910589"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8658a448-6b64-4c00-a127-6518152fb667"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Establishment of a Novel Human Fetal Adrenal Culture Model that Supports de Novo and Manipulated Steroidogenesis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-473792","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Despite significant evidence that the expression of several microRNAs (miRNAs) impacts disease activity in patients with ulcerative colitis (UC), it remains unknown if the more severe disease phenotype seen in pediatric onset UC can be explained by an altered miRNA expression. In this study, we assessed the relationship between miRNA expression, age, and disease severity in pediatric and adult patients with UC. Using RT-qPCR, we analyzed the expression of miR-21, miR-31, miR-126, miR-142 and miR-155 in paraffin embedded rectum biopsies from 30 pediatric and 30 adult-onset UC patients. We found that lesions from adult patients had significantly higher expression levels of miR-21 compared to pediatric patients and that the expression levels of miR-31 (all patients) and miR-155 (pediatric patients only) correlated inversely with histological assessed disease severity. Using in situ hybridization followed by image analysis, the expression level estimates of miR-21 and miR-126 correlated with histological assessed disease severity. In conclusion, we found that the expression of miRNAs depends on the age of the patient and/or the severity of the disease, suggesting that miRNAs may contribute to the regulation of inflammation in UC and could be useful biomarkers in the surveillance of disease severity.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Malham, Mikkel","James, Jaslin P.","Jakobsen, Christian","Høgdall, Estrid","Holmstroem, Kim","Wewer, Vibeke","Nielsen, Boye S.","Riis, Lene Buhl"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-507958"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["In situ hybridization","Inflammatory bowel disease","Pediatric","Quantitative polymerase chain reaction","miRNA"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GTS","DI-U"],"level3":{"GTS":["DI-GTS-ALL","DI-GTS-BION"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GTS-BION","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"mucosal micrornas relate to age and severity of disease in ulcerative colitis","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.18632/aging.202715","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-507958","identifier":[{"id":"85102817662","type":"scopus"},{"id":"33647883","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC7993741","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-bfe9f131-b2bc-40b0-a6c0-d0ec922d1778"],"keyword":["in situ hybridization","inflammatory bowel disease","miRNA","pediatric","quantitative polymerase chain reaction"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1DAFAE64C3EA4BCC5944C81890549152"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/282195576/aging_v13i5_202715.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.18632/aging.202715","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"bioneer","l2":"DI-GTS","l2n":"GTS Institutes","l3":"DI-GTS-BION","l3nn":"Bioneer","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Bioneer","namevar":["Bioneer"],"nora_code":"DI-GTS-BION","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Mikkel","key":"malhampau","last":"Malham","name":"Malham, Mikkel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jaslin P.","key":"jamespau","last":"James","locid":"db7a60ca-ab78-4f9f-980c-5ca8309b9ce6","name":"James, Jaslin P.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Christian","key":"jakobsenpau","last":"Jakobsen","name":"Jakobsen, Christian","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Estrid","key":"hoegdallpau","last":"Hoegdall","locid":"a48f77b8-ee6d-4a52-a3f3-deb515bdd48f","name":"Høgdall, Estrid","name_org":"Hoegdall, Estrid","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Kim","key":"holmstroempau","last":"Holmstroem","name":"Holmstroem, Kim","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Vibeke","key":"wewerpau","last":"Wewer","name":"Wewer, Vibeke","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Boye S.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Boye S.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lene B.","key":"riispau","last":"Riis","locid":"8ec061c0-f464-42fd-ac8d-f972f86292cd","name":"Riis, Lene Buhl","name_org":"Riis, Lene B.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33647883"],"pub":{"doi":"10.18632/aging.202715","issn":["19454589"],"issue":"5","journal":"Aging","pages":"6359-6374","status":"p","title":"Aging","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85102817662"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All GTS Institutes","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GTS","DI-GTS-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","GTS Institutes","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-bfe9f131-b2bc-40b0-a6c0-d0ec922d1778"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Mucosal microRNAs relate to age and severity of disease in ulcerative colitis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-507958","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: Disordered fetal adrenal steroidogenesis can cause marked clinical effects including virilization of female fetuses. In postnatal life, adrenal disorders can be life-threatening due to the risk of adrenal crisis and must be carefully managed. However, testing explicit adrenal steroidogenic inhibitory effects of therapeutic drugs is challenging due to species-specific characteristics, and particularly the impact of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) stimulation on drugs targeting steroidogenesis has not previously been examined in human adrenal tissue. Therefore, this study aimed to examine the effects of selected steroidogenic inhibitors on human fetal adrenal (HFA) steroid hormone production under basal and ACTH-stimulated conditions. Methods: This study used an established HFA ex vivo culture model to examine treatment effects in 78 adrenals from 50 human fetuses (gestational weeks 8–12). Inhibitors were selected to affect enzymes critical for different steps in classic adrenal steroidogenic pathways, including CYP17A1 (Abiraterone acetate), CYP11B1/2 (Osilodrostat), and a suggested CYP21A2 inhibitor (Efavirenz). Treatment effects were examined under basal and ACTH-stimulated conditions in tissue from the same fetus and determined by quantifying the secretion of adrenal steroids in the culture media using liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry. Statistical analysis was performed on ln-transformed data using one-way ANOVA for repeated measures followed by Tukey’s multiple comparisons test. Results: Treatment with Abiraterone acetate and Osilodrostat resulted in potent inhibition of CYP17A1 and CYP11B1/2, respectively, while treatment with Efavirenz reduced testosterone secretion under basal conditions. ACTH-stimulation affected the inhibitory effects of all investigated drugs. Thus, treatment effects of Abiraterone acetate were more pronounced under stimulated conditions, while Efavirenz treatment caused a non-specific inhibition on steroidogenesis. ACTH-stimulation prevented the Osilodrostat-mediated CYP11B1 inhibition observed under basal conditions. Conclusions: Our results show that the effects of steroidogenic inhibitors differ under basal and ACTH-stimulated conditions in the HFA ex vivo culture model. This could suggest that in vivo effects of therapeutic drugs targeting steroidogenesis may vary in conditions where patients have suppressed or high ACTH levels, respectively. This study further demonstrates that ex vivo cultured HFAs can be used to evaluate steroidogenic inhibitors and thereby provide novel information about the local effects of existing and emerging drugs that targets steroidogenesis.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Melau, Cecilie","Riis, Malene Lundgaard","Nielsen, John E.","Perlman, Signe","Lundvall, Lene","Thuesen, Lea Langhoff","Juul Hare, Kristine","Hammerum, Mette Schou","Mitchell, Rod T.","Frederiksen, Hanne","Juul, Anders","Jørgensen, Anne"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-513432"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Edinburgh"],"keyword":["ACTH","Abiraterone","Adrenal","Efavirenz","Ex vivo","Human","Steroid hormones"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the effects of selected inhibitors on human fetal adrenal steroidogenesis differs under basal and acth stimulated conditions","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12916-021-02080-8","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-513432","identifier":[{"id":"85114411736","type":"scopus"},{"i