ua","rank":21,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","first":"Mamoun","key":"younesmamounpau","last":"Younes","name":"Younes, Mamoun","rank":22,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","first":"Andrew","key":"dupontandrewpau","last":"DuPont","name":"DuPont, Andrew","rank":23,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Selvi","key":"thirumurthiselvipau","last":"Thirumurthi","name":"Thirumurthi, Selvi","rank":24,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Eduardo","key":"vilareduardopau","last":"Vilar","name":"Vilar, Eduardo","rank":25,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Mary K.","key":"estesmarykpau","last":"Estes","name":"Estes, Mary K.","rank":26,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Simona","key":"collasimonapau","last":"Colla","name":"Colla, Simona","rank":27,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Noah F.","key":"shroyernoahfpau","last":"Shroyer","name":"Shroyer, Noah F.","rank":28,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ronald A.","key":"depinhoronaldapau","last":"DePinho","name":"DePinho, Ronald A.","rank":29,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34253611"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1073/pnas.2024853118","issn":["00278424","10916490"],"issn_org":["00278424"],"issue":"29","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","paperid":"e2024853118","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","volume":"118"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85109879044"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-0ea17d11-5df2-49bc-9484-8a689994b7c9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Telomere dysfunction instigates inflammation in inflammatory bowel disease","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-402134","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Introduction: Evidence about the consequences of severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) infection in pregnancy is rapidly increasing; however, data on antibody response and risk of transmission during pregnancy and delivery are still limited. The aim of this study was to evaluate if SARS-CoV-2 is detectable in vaginal swabs and whether antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 are present in maternal and umbilical cord blood of pregnant women with confirmed SARS-CoV-2. Material and methods: A single-unit prospective cohort study in Denmark including pregnant women with SARS-CoV-2 infection confirmed by a pharyngeal swab between August 20, 2020, and March 1, 2021, who gave birth during the same period. All patients admitted to the maternity ward and antepartum clinic were screened for SARS-CoV-2 infection. A maternal blood sample and vaginal swabs were collected at inclusion. If included antepartum, these samples were repeated intrapartum when an umbilical cord blood sample was also collected. Swabs were analyzed for SARS-CoV-2 and blood samples were analyzed for SARS-CoV-2 total antibodies. Placental and neonatal swabs as well as placental histopathological examinations were performed on clinical indications. Results: We included 28 women, of whom four had serious maternal or fetal outcomes including one case of neonatal death. Within the first 8 days after confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection, SARS-CoV-2 was detectable in two vaginal swabs (2/28) and SARS-CoV-2 antibodies were detected in 1 of 13 women. From 16 days after confirmed infection, antibodies were observed in 19 of 21 of women. Antibodies in cord blood were not detected during the first 16 days after confirmed infection (n = 7). However, from 26 days, antibodies were present in 16 of 17 cord blood samples of seropositive mothers. Placental examination in two cases of severe fetal outcomes preceded by reduced fetal movements revealed SARS-CoV-2 in swabs and severe histopathological abnormalities. Conclusions: SARS-CoV-2 was detected in only 2 of 28 vaginal swabs within 8 days after confirmed infection in pregnant women. Our data suggest that maternal seroconversion occurs between days 8 and 16, whereas antibodies in cord blood of seropositive mothers were present in the majority from 26 days after confirmed infection. Additional data are needed regarding timing of seroconversion for the mother and appearance of antibodies in cord blood.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Milbak, Julie","Holten, Victoria M.F.","Axelsson, Paul Bryde","Bendix, Jane Marie","Aabakke, Anna J.M.","Nielsen, Lene","Friis, Martin Barfred","Jensen, Claus A.J.","Løkkegaard, Ellen","Olsen, Tina Elisabeth","Rode, Line","Clausen, Tine Dalsgaard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-429625"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cohort Studies","Coronavirus disease 2019","Placental dysfunction","Pregnancy Complications","Pregnancy Outcome","Prospective Studies","Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2","Vertical transmission","obstetric delivery"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a prospective cohort study of confirmed severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 000000000002 sars cov 000000000002 infection during pregnancy evaluating sars cov 000000000002 antibodies in maternal and umbilical cord blood and sars cov 000000000002 in vaginal swabs","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/aogs.14274","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-429625","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8653183","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"34719780","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85118312567","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-3c8c6f1c-7f04-4fc8-ae04-36b5d842154e"],"keyword":["cohort studies","coronavirus disease 2019","obstetric delivery","placental dysfunction","pregnancy complications","pregnancy outcome","prospective studies","severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2","vertical transmission"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9FFDFF2E25E7F7FE7364B50B75166AFE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/aogs.14274","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/285871204/aogs.14274.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital – North Zealand"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Holbæk Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"nora_code"