,"type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.69-007303","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Herein, we characterize the cellular uptake of a DNA structure generated by rolling circle DNA amplification. The structure, termed nanoflower, was fluorescently labeled by incorporation of ATTO488-dUTP allowing the intracellular localization to be followed. The nanoflower had a hydrodynamic diameter of approximately 300 nanometer and was non-toxic for all mammalian cell lines tested. It was internalized specifically by mammalian macrophages by phagocytosis within a few hours resulting in specific compartmentalization in phagolysosomes. Maximum uptake was observed after eight hours and the nanoflower remained stable in the phagolysosomes with a half-life of 12 h. Interestingly, the nanoflower co-localized with both Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Leishmania infantum within infected macrophages although these pathogens escape lysosomal degradation by affecting the phagocytotic pathway in very different manners. These results suggest an intriguing and overlooked potential application of DNA structures in targeted treatment of infectious diseases such as tuberculosis and leishmaniasis that are caused by pathogens that escape the human immune system by modifying macrophage biology.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Franch, Oskar","Gutiérrez-Corbo, Camino","Domínguez-Asenjo, Bárbara","Boesen, Thomas","Jensen, Pia Bomholt","Nejsum, Lene Niemann","Keller, Josephine Geertsen","Nielsen, Simon Pagaard","Singh, Prakruti R","Jha, Rajiv Kumar","Nagaraja, Valakunja","Balaña-Fouce, Rafael","Ho, Yi-Ping","Reguera, Rosa María","Knudsen, Birgitta R."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-249703"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["Hong Kong"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Chinese University of Hong Kong"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dna flowerstructure co localizes with human pathogens in infected macrophages","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkaa341","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-249703","identifier":[{"id":"PMC7293011","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85086523557","type":"scopus"},{"id":"32402089","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-d2bd790f-e247-4056-9f4c-9c1cd4b9eef4"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B82051272D73FD4330EEE279BD878E9C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkaa341","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics, Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics - Genome stability and technology","Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"hk","key":"chinese university of hong kong","loc":["Hong Kong","Asia, East"],"name":"Chinese University of Hong Kong","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oskar","key":"franchpau","last":"Franch","locid":"115cded8-bf4b-40ad-a8bf-d8c1bb2f4a80","