{"abstract":"Shea (Vitellaria paradoxa) is an important fruit tree in West African parklands, and its successful pollination is a requirement for fruit production. Size-based pollinator exclusion experiments combined with visual observations showed that presence of honey bees (Apis mellifera jemenitica) was important for pollination and thereby the production of fruits and seeds. Smaller insects, mainly species of stingless bees (Hypotrigona spp. and Liotrigona cf. bottegoi) and solitary bees (Compsomelissa borneri) could partly compensate pollination in absence of honey bees, but fertilisation and fruit yield was reduced. A positive correlation between fertilisation percentage and number of honey bee colonies within radii of 900 and 1000 m was observed. The percentage of fertilisation and number of mature fruits per fascicle were higher in trees with colonies of stingless bees in the trunk when honey bees were excluded by bagging. We conclude that local beekeeping with honey bees and stingless bees is likely to have a positive influence on fruit production of shea trees in the farmed West African parklands, which speaks in favour of a pollinator friendly environment.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Lassen, Kristin Marie","Nielsen, Lene Rostgaard","Dupont, Yoko Luise","Lompo, Djingdia","Kjær, Erik Dahl"],"cuid":["S02-099.71-012832"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Africa"],"country":["Burkina Faso"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Centre national de semences forestières"],"keyword":["Agroforestry parklands","Honey bees","Pollination","Solitary bees","Stingless bees","Vitellaria paradoxa"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"honey bees are essential for pollination of em vitellaria paradoxa em subsp em paradoxa em sapotaceae in burkina faso","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84983399875","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-099.71-012832","identifier":[{"id":"84983399875","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-0349a392-6e8f-4a5a-b05f-6d6f93d67a6e","ku-e7fb1efb-eb2c-48ff-ba79-98b4a1d7c02e"],"keyword":["Agroforestry parklands","Honey bees","Pollination","Solitary bees","Stingless bees","Vitellaria paradoxa"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-6777B51E980C75010092333849B38E78"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Ecoscience, Department of Ecoscience - Terrestrial Ecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"bf","key":"centre national de semences forestières","loc":["Burkina Faso","Africa"],"name":"Centre national de semences forestières","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kristin Marie","key":"lassenpau","last":"Lassen","name":"Lassen, Kristin Marie","orcid":"0000-0003-3274-4875","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene Rostgaard","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Lene Rostgaard","orcid":"0000-0002-7214-8691","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Yoko Luise","key":"dupontpau","last":"Dupont","locid":"2a165175-a558-448c-9b2e-8fe1150a0fac","name":"Dupont, Yoko Luise","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Djingdia","key":"lompopau","last":"Lompo","name":"Lompo, Djingdia","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Erik Dahl","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","name":"Kjær, Erik Dahl","orcid":"0000-0001-8624-1611","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10457-016-0007-z","issn":["01674366","15729680"],"issn_org":["01674366"],"issue":"1","journal":"Agroforestry Systems","pages":"23-34","publisher":"Springer Science and Business Media BV","publisher_org":"Springer Science and Business Media B.V.","status":"p","title":"Agroforestry Systems","volume":"92"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84983399875"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-0349a392-6e8f-4a5a-b05f-6d6f93d67a6e","ku-e7fb1efb-eb2c-48ff-ba79-98b4a1d7c02e"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Honey bees are essential for pollination of Vitellaria paradoxa subsp. paradoxa (Sapotaceae) in Burkina Faso","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.71-012832","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Background: Biting midges of the genus Culicoides (Diptera: Ceratopogonidae) are small hematophagous insects responsible for the transmission of bluetongue virus, Schmallenberg virus and African horse sickness virus to wild and domestic ruminants and equids. Outbreaks of these viruses have caused economic damage within the European Union. The spatio-temporal distribution of biting midges is a key factor in identifying areas with the potential for disease spread. The aim of this study was to identify and map areas of neglectable adult activity for each month in an average year. Average monthly risk maps can be used as a tool when allocating resources for surveillance and control programs within Europe. Methods: We modelled the occurrence of C. imicola and the Obsoletus and Pulicaris ensembles using existing entomological surveillance data from Spain, France, Germany, Switzerland, Austria, Denmark, Sweden, Norway and Poland. The monthly probability of each vector species and ensembles being present in Europe based on climatic and environmental input variables was estimated with the machine learning technique Random Forest. Subsequently, the monthly probability was classified into three classes: Absence, Presence and Uncertain status. These three classes are useful for mapping areas of no risk, areas of high-risk targeted for animal movement restrictions, and areas with an uncertain status that need active entomological surveillance to determine whether or not vectors are present. Results: The distribution of Culicoides species ensembles were in agreement with their previously reported distribution in Europe. The Random Forest models were very accurate in predicting the probability of presence for C. imicola (mean AUC = 0.95), less accurate for the Obsoletus ensemble (mean AUC = 0.84), while the lowest accuracy was found for the Pulicaris ensemble (mean AUC = 0.71). The most important environmental variables in the models were related to temperature and precipitation for all three groups. Conclusions: The duration periods with low or null adult activity can be derived from the associated monthly distribution maps, and it was also possible to identify and map areas with uncertain predictions. In the absence of ongoing vector surveillance, these maps can be used by veterinary authorities to classify areas as likely vector-free or as likely risk areas from southern Spain to northern Sweden with acceptable precision. The maps can also focus costly entomological surveillance to seasons and areas where the predictions and vector-free status remain uncertain.","author_count":42,"contributors":["Cuéllar, Ana Carolina","Jung Kjær, Lene","Baum, Andreas","Stockmarr, Anders","Skovgård, Henrik","Nielsen, Søren Achim","Andersson, Mats Gunnar","Lindström, Anders","Chirico, Jan","Lühken, Renke","Steinke, Sonja","Kiel, Ellen","Gethmann, Jörn","Conraths, Franz J.","Larska, Magdalena","Smreczak, Marcin","Orłowska, Anna","Hamnes, Inger","Sviland, Ståle","Hopp, Petter","Brugger, Katharina","Rubel, Franz","Balenghien, Thomas","Garros, Claire","Rakotoarivony, Ignace","Allène, Xavier","Lhoir, Jonathan","Chavernac, David","Delécolle, Jean Claude","Mathieu, Bruno","Delécolle, Delphine","Setier-Rio, Marie Laure","Venail, Roger","Scheid, Bethsabée","Chueca, Miguel Ángel Miranda","Barceló, Carlos","Lucientes, Javier","Estrada, Rosa","Mathis, Alexander","Tack, Wesley","Bødker, René"],"cuid":["S03-095.20-025207"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Cuellar, Ana Carolina","Cuéllar, Ana Carolina"],"country":["Austria","Belgium","France","Germany","Norway","Poland","Spain","Sweden","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Avia-GIS","Avia-GIS NV","Bernhard Nocht Institute for Tropical Medicine","CIRAD","CIRAD - The French Agricultural Research Centre for International Dev