{"author_count":2,"contributors":["Nellemann, Annette Nielsen","Seibæk, Lene"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-192098"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"pop","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sygeplejerskens støtte er afgørende for barnløse","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-192098","ids":["au-6dfcdb87-108f-4279-8f70-28ed3c9e877d"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Popular","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-D661165B5D1B42F97CDCAFCC50375E7E"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Annette Nielsen","key":"nellemannpau","last":"Nellemann","name":"Nellemann, Annette Nielsen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"seibækpau","last":"Seibæk","locid":"2bf95667-24b9-444b-b3e1-a62965aac67a","name":"Seibæk, Lene","orcid":"0000-0002-2396-8669","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["24459461"],"journal":"Fag & Forskning","publisher":"Dansk Sygeplejeråd","status":"p","title":"Fag & Forskning","title_org":"Fag og Forskning","volume":"1"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-6dfcdb87-108f-4279-8f70-28ed3c9e877d"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Sygeplejerskens støtte er afgørende for barnløse","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-192098","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Polyketide synthases (PKSs) and nonribosomal peptide synthetases (NRPSs) each give rise to a vast array of complex bioactive molecules with further complexity added by the existence of natural PKS-NRPS fusions. Rational genetic engineering for the production of natural product derivatives is desirable for the purpose of incorporating new functionalities into pre-existing molecules, or for optimization of known bioactivities. We sought to expand the range of natural product diversity by combining modules of PKS-NRPS hybrids from different hosts, hereby producing novel synthetic natural products. We succeeded in the construction of a functional cross-species chimeric PKS-NRPS expressed in Aspergillus nidulans. Module swapping of the two PKS-NRPS natural hybrids CcsA from Aspergillus clavatus involved in the biosynthesis of cytochalasin E and related Syn2 from rice plant pathogen Magnaporthe oryzae lead to production of novel hybrid products, demonstrating that the rational re-design of these fungal natural product enzymes is feasible. We also report the structure of four novel pseudo pre-cytochalasin intermediates, niduclavin and niduporthin along with the chimeric compounds niduchimaeralin A and B, all indicating that PKS-NRPS activity alone is insufficient for proper assembly of the cytochalasin core structure. Future success in the field of biocombinatorial synthesis of hybrid polyketide-nonribosomal peptides relies on the understanding of the fundamental mechanisms of inter-modular polyketide chain transfer. Therefore, we expressed several PKS-NRPS linker-modified variants. Intriguingly, the linker anatomy is less complex than expected, as these variants displayed great tolerance with regards to content and length, showing a hitherto unreported flexibility in PKS-NRPS hybrids, with great potential for synthetic biology-driven biocombinatorial chemistry.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Nielsen, Maria Lund","Isbrandt, Thomas","Petersen, Lene Maj","Mortensen, Uffe Hasbro","Andersen, Mikael Rørdam","Hoof, Jakob Blæsbjerg","Larsen, Thomas Ostenfeld"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-341402"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"linker flexibility facilitates module exchange in fungal hybrid pks nrps engineering","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0161199","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-341402","identifier":[{"id":"84984853918","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC4994942","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"27551732","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-7b778094-b9f6-4f55-a025-87099d8b412d"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-216682D6540C92EE2C8A6493ADFF2DC3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/125669092/Linker_Flexibility_Facilitates_Module_Exchange_in_Fungal_Hybrid_PKS_NRPS_Engineering.PDF","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0161199","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Systems Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Maria Lund","key":"nielsenmarialundpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"3161157c-75e3-4aa2-8376-8fbc8cb8b1ee","name":"Nielsen, Maria Lund","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas Isbrandt","key":"isbrandtthomaspau","last":"Petersen","locid":"0a0ca6df-f39c-4d4f-b7ce-d476ea20cc7d","name":"Isbrandt, Thomas","name_org":"Petersen, Thomas Isbrandt","orcid":"0000-0003-3938-0816","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lene Maj","key":"petersenlenemajpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"7a1c5a02-7eb8-40e3-a1ec-621fb3c37652","name":"Petersen, Lene Maj","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Uffe Hasbro","key":"mortensenuffehasbropau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"dec3038b-f004-4c96-84a1-52825e1c0ce8","name":"Mortensen, Uffe Hasbro","orcid":"0000-0002-7794-7273","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mikael Rørdam","key":"andersenmikaelrørdampau","last":"Andersen","locid":"2b65dbe3-5af3-4d59-84ef-d999b36e5376","name":"Andersen, Mikael Rørdam","orcid":"0000-0003-4794-6808","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jakob Blæsbjerg","key":"hoofjakobblæsbjergpau","last":"Hoof","locid":"339ce5a8-3232-410d-9484-abbc0f5b81b3","name":"Hoof, Jakob Blæsbjerg","orcid":"0000-0002-7650-9300","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas Ostenfeld","key":"larsenthomasostenfeldpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"bd958d52-71b6-4720-b30a-cc9305633b43","name":"Larsen, Thomas Ostenfeld","orcid":"0000-0002-3362-5707","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27551732"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0161199","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"8","journal":"PLOS ONE","paperid":"e0161199","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","title_org":"P L o S One","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84984853918"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-7b778094-b9f6-4f55-a025-87099d8b412d"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Linker Flexibility Facilitates Module Exchange in Fungal Hybrid PKS-NRPS Engineering","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-341402","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: In March 2008, the D:A:D study published results demonstrating an increased risk of myocardial infarction (MI) for patients on abacavir (ABC). We describe changes to the use of ABC since this date, and investigate changes to the association between ABC and MI with subsequent follow-up. METHODS: A total of 49,717 D:A:D participants were followed from study entry until the first of an MI, death, 1 February 2013 or 6 months after last visit. Associations between a person's 10-year cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk and the likelihood of initiating or discontinuing ABC were assessed using multivariable logistic/Poisson regression. Poisson regression was used to assess the association between current ABC use and MI risk, adjusting for potential confounders, and a test of interaction was performed to assess whether the association had changed in the post-March 2008 period. RESULTS: Use of ABC increased from 10 % of the cohort in 2000 to 20 % in 2008, before stabilising at 18-19 %. Increases in use pre-March 2008, and subsequent decreases, were greatest in those at moderate and high CVD risk. Post-March 2008, those on ABC at moderate/high CVD risk were more likely to discontinue ABC than those at low/unknown CVD risk, regardless of viral load (≤1,000 copies/ml: relative rate 1.49 [95 % confidence interval 1.34-1.65]; >1,000 copies/ml: 1.23 [1.02-1.48]); no such associations were seen pre-March 2008. There was some evidence that antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve persons at moderate/high CVD risk post-March 2008 were less likely to initiate ABC than those at low/unknown CVD risk (odds ratio 0.74 [0.48-1.13]). By 1 February 2013, 941 MI events had occurred in 367,559 person-years. Current ABC use was associated with a 98 % increase in MI rate (RR 1.98 [1.72-2.29]) with no difference in the pre- (1.97 [1.68-2.33]) or post- (1.97 [1.43-2.72]) March 2008 periods (interaction P = 0.74). CONCLUSIONS: Despite a reduction in the channelling of ABC for patients at higher CVD risk since 2008, we continue to observe an association between ABC use and MI risk. Whilst confounding cannot be fully ruled out, this further diminishes channelling bias as an explanation for our findings.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Sabin, Caroline A","Reiss, Peter","Nielsen, Lene Ryom","Phillips, Andrew N","Weber, Rainer","Law, Matthew","Fontas, Eric","Mocroft, Amanda","de Wit, Stephane","Smith, Colette","Dabis, Francois","d'Arminio Monforte, Antonella","El-Sadr, Wafaa","Lundgren, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-403177"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corpau":["D:A:D Study Group"],"keyword":["Adult","Anti HIV Agents","Australia","Dideoxynucleosides","Europe","Female","HIV Infections","Humans","Logistic Models","Male","Medication Therapy Management","Middle Aged","Myocardial Infarction","Observational Study","Odds Ratio","Pharmacovigilance","Practice Patterns Physicians","Risk Assessment","Risk Factors","United States"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"is there continued evidence for an association between abacavir usage and myocardial infarction risk in individuals with hiv a cohort collaboration","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12916-016-0588-4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-403177","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4815070","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"27036962","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84977672968","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-106da6e3-ff96-4952-ab9d-3c8cf9e6f97b"],"keyword":["Adult","Anti-HIV Agents","Australia","Dideoxynucleosides","Europe","Female","HIV Infections","Humans","Logistic Models","Male","Medication Therapy Management","Middle Aged","Myocardial Infarction","Observational Study","Odds Ratio","Pharmacovigilance","Practice Patterns, Physicians'","Risk Assessment","Risk Factors","United States"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1EA69EAAD668D64B9BB0124598A22F87"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12916-016-0588-4","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/173098683/art_3A10.1186_2Fs12916_016_0588_4.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Immunology and Microbiology, CHIP"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":2,"key":"d a d study group","name":"D:A:D Study Group","role":"oau"}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Caroline A","key":"sabinpau","last":"Sabin","name":"Sabin, Caroline A","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Peter","key":"reisspau","last":"Reiss","name":"Reiss, Peter","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lene","key":"ryompau","last":"Ryom","locid":"0087fd9d-3a4d-4da5-9f4d-2ba51ec7d7a8","name":"Nielsen, Lene Ryom","name_org":"Ryom, Lene","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Andrew N","key":"phillipspau","last":"Phillips","name":"Phillips, Andrew N","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Rainer","key":"weberpau","last":"Weber","name":"Weber, Rainer","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Matthew","key":"lawpau","last":"Law","name":"Law, Matthew","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Eric","key":"fontaspau","last":"Fontas","name":"Fontas, Eric","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Amanda","key":"mocroftpau","last":"Mocroft","name":"Mocroft, Amanda","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Stephane","key":"dewitpau","last":"de Wit","name":"de Wit, Stephane","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Colette","key":"smithpau","last":"Smith","name":"Smith, Colette","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Francois","key":"dabispau","last":"Dabis","name":"Dabis, Francois","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Antonella","key":"darminiomonfortepau","last":"d'Arminio Monforte","name":"d'Arminio Monforte, Antonella","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Wafaa","key":"elsadrpau","last":"El-Sadr","name":"El-Sadr, Wafaa","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jens D","key":"lundgrenpau","last":"Lundgren","locid":"af310f7a-c49b-4381-bcb4-cf56e3fd80d8","name":"Lundgren, Jens","name_org":"Lundgren, Jens D","orcid":"0000-0001-8901-7850","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27036962"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12916-016-0588-4","issn":["17417015"],"journal":"BMC Medicine","paperid":"61","status":"p","title":"BMC Medicine","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84977672968"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-106da6e3-ff96-4952-ab9d-3c8cf9e6f97b"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Is there continued evidence for an association between abacavir usage and myocardial infarction risk in individuals with HIV? : A cohort collaboration","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-403177","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: The European AIDS Clinical Society (EACS) guidelines are intended for all clinicians involved in the care of HIV-positive persons, and are available in print, online, and as a free App for download for iPhone and Android. GUIDELINE HIGHLIGHTS: The 2015 version of the EACS guidelines contains major revisions in all sections; antiretroviral treatment (ART), comorbidities, coinfections and opportunistic diseases. Among the key revisions is the recommendation of ART for all HIV-positive persons, irrespectively of CD4 count, based on the Strategic Timing of AntiRetroviral Treatment (START) study results. The recommendations for the preferred and the alternative ART options have also been revised, and a new section on the use of pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) has been added. A number of new antiretroviral drugs/drug combinations have been added to the updated tables on drug-drug interactions, adverse drug effects, dose adjustment for renal/liver insufficiency and for ART administration in persons with swallowing difficulties. The revisions of the coinfection section reflect the major advances in anti-hepatitis C virus (HCV) treatment with direct-acting antivirals with earlier start of treatment in individuals at increased risk of liver disease progression, and a phasing out of interferon-containing treatment regimens. The section on opportunistic diseases has been restructured according to individual pathogens/diseases and a new overview table has been added on CD4 count thresholds for different primary prophylaxes. CONCLUSIONS: The diagnosis and management of HIV infection and related coinfections, opportunistic diseases and comorbidities continue to require a multidisciplinary effort for which the 2015 version of the EACS guidelines provides an easily accessable and updated overview.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Nielsen, Lene Ryom","Boesecke, C","Gisler, V","Manzardo, C","Rockstroh, J K","Puoti, M","Furrer, H","Miro, J M","Gatell, J M","