{"abstract":"Background: The impact of surgical approach on the incidence of small bowel obstruction (SBO) is unclear. The aim of the current study was to analyze the long-term risk of surgery for SBO after open and laparoscopic surgery and to assess how subsequent SBO surgery impacts on mortality after colonic cancer resection. Methods: This was a nationwide cohort study of patients undergoing elective colonic cancer resection with primary anastomosis in Denmark between 2001 and 2008. All included patients were operated with curative intent. Patients were identified in the Danish Colorectal Cancer Group database and followed through May 2014 in the Danish National Patient Register. The primary endpoint was surgery for SBO. Secondarily, mortality among patients who subsequently underwent SBO surgery and those who did not was compared. Results: Among the 8583 included patients, 251 (2.9 %) underwent surgery for SBO during follow-up (median 8.8 years). The 3-year cumulative incidence of SBO surgery was 1.5 %; 1.2 % after laparoscopic and 1.6 % after open surgery. Laparoscopic surgery was associated with a decreased risk of SBO (hazard ratio [HR] 0.61 (CI 0.37 to 0.99, P = 0.048) compared with open surgery. The HR for mortality after colonic resection was 2.54 (CI 1.91 to 3.38, P < 0.001) for patients who underwent subsequent SBO surgery as compared to those who did not. Conclusions: Laparoscopic surgery for colonic cancer was associated with a decreased risk of subsequent SBO surgery compared with open surgery. Further, subsequent SBO surgery was associated with increased mortality after colonic cancer resection.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Jensen, Kristian Kiim","Erichsen, Rune","Scheike, Thomas","Iversen, Lene Hjerrild","Krarup, Peter-Martin","Andersen, Peter"],"cuid":["S02-094.78-024497"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Adhesions","Complications","Minimally invasive","Mortality"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"decreased risk of surgery for small bowel obstruction after laparoscopic colon cancer surgery compared with open surgery a nationwide cohort study","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84964614124","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-094.78-024497","identifier":[{"id":"84964614124","type":"scopus"},{"id":"27129566","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-a191c2d4-1b97-4ee1-9248-db450e55d3c0","ku-7944f777-1486-4b79-9424-e60927e2f92c"],"keyword":["Adhesions","Complications","Minimally invasive","Mortality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-BE8CE476AD089BA1539985020A959032"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Bispebjerg og Frederiksberg Universitets Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Surgical Gastroenterology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Kristian Kiim","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"cf0c3470-b93a-415c-ba19-aae2c6111c3d","name":"Jensen, Kristian K.","name_org":"Jensen, Kristian Kiim","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Rune","key":"erichsenpau","last":"Erichsen","locid":"6ca944c9-6ee4-4ee6-abdb-3bb92452e5cc","name":"Erichsen, Rune","orcid":"0000-0001-9398-9185","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lene Hjerrild","key":"iversenpau","last":"Iversen","locid":"4d8ada79-98c2-49eb-ac2f-472b41434eff","name":"Iversen, Lene Hjerrild","orcid":"0000-0002-2336-2923","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas","key":"scheikepau","last":"Scheike","locid":"53de2123-e34e-44a3-945f-265fce28de25","name":"Scheike, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0002-2148-4740","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter-Martin","key":"kraruppau","last":"Krarup","name":"Krarup, Peter-Martin","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"54d2ee21-ebb1-4459-9848-0d2e18ad859f","name":"Andersen, Peter","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Martin Krarup","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Martin Krarup","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27129566"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00464-016-4930-x","issn":["09302794","14322218"],"issn_org":["09302794"],"issue":"12","journal":"Surgical Endoscopy","pages":"1-11","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Surgical Endoscopy","volume":"30"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84964614124"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-a191c2d4-1b97-4ee1-9248-db450e55d3c0","ku-7944f777-1486-4b79-9424-e60927e2f92c"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Decreased risk of surgery for small bowel obstruction after laparoscopic colon cancer surgery compared with open surgery : a nationwide cohort study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-094.78-024497","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Context: NNC0195-0092 is a reversible, albumin-bindingGHderivative, developed for once-weekly administration. Objectives: The objective of the study was to evaluate safety, local tolerability, pharmacodynamics, and pharmacokinetics of multiple, once-weekly doses of NNC0195-0092, compared with daily GH. Design and Setting: This was a phase 1, randomized, open-label, active-controlled, multiple-dose, dose-escalation trial. Patients: Thirty-four GH-treated adult subjects (male,n=25) withGHdeficiency participated in the study. Interventions and Main Outcome Measures: Subjects were sequentially assigned into four cohorts of eight subjects, randomized within each cohort (3:1) to once-weekly NNC0195-0092 (n = 6) for 4 weeks (0.02, 0.04, 0.08, and 0.12 mg/kg) or daily injections of Norditropin NordiFlex (n=2) for 4 weeks with a dose replicating the pretrial dose of somatropin. A safety assessment was performed prior to initiating treatment at the next dose level of NNC0195-0092. Daily GH treatment was discontinued 14 days before the trial start. Blood samples were drawn for assessment of safety, pharmacokinetics, pharmacodynamics (IGF-1 and IGF-binding protein-3) profiles, and immunogenicity studies. Results: Numbers of adverse events were similar at the dose levels of 0.02, 0.04, and 0.08 mg/kg NNC0195-0092 vs daily injections of Norditropin NordiFlex, whereas the number of adverse events was greater at the highest dose level of NNC0195-0092 (0.12 mg/kg). NNC0195-0092 (area under the curve[0-168h]) and peak plasma concentration) increased in a dose-dependent manner, and a dose-dependent increase in IGF-1 levels was observed. IGF-1 profiles were elevated for at least 1 week, and for the 0.02-mg/kg and 0.04-mg/kg NNC0195-0092 doses, the observed IGF-1 levels were similar to the levels for the active control group. Conclusion: Four once-weekly doses of NNC0195-0092 (dose range 0.02-0.12 mg/kg) administered to adultpatientswithGHdeficiencywerewelltolerated,andIGF-1profileswereconsistentwithaonce-weekly treatment profile. No clinically significant safety and tolerability signals causally related to NNC0195-0092 were identified, nor were any immunogenicity concerns revealed.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Michael Højby","Janukonyté, Jurgita","Klose, Marianne","Marina, Djordje","Tanvig, Mette","Nielsen, Lene F","Höybye, Charlotte","Andersen, Marianne","Feldt-Rasmussen, Ulla","Christiansen, Jens Sandahl"],"cuid":["S03-097.98-027090"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karolinska Institute"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Comparative Study","Dose Response Relationship Drug","Female","Human Growth Hormone","Human Growth Hormone administration dosage","Humans","Insulin Like Growth Factor Binding Protein 3","Insulin Like Growth Factor Binding Protein 3 blood","Insulin Like Growth Factor I","Journal Article","Lipopeptides","Male","Middle Aged","Prospective Studies","Randomized Controlled Trial","Research Support Non U S Gov t"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR"],"OT":["MEA, Aarhus"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOVO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-PR-NOVO","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reversible albumin binding gh possesses a potential once weekly treatment profile in adult growth hormone deficiency","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2015-1991","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-097.98-027090","identifier":[{"id":