{"abstract":"Polyploidy is a common phenomenon in the evolution of angiosperms. It has been suggested that polyploids manage harsh environments better than their diploid relatives but empirical data supporting this hypothesis are scarce, especially for trees. Using microsatellite markers and flow cytometry, we examine the frequency of polyploids and diploids in a progeny trial testing four different populations of Acacia senegal, a species native to sub-Saharan regions of Africa. We compare growth between cytotypes and test whether polyploid seedlings grow better than diploids. Our results show that polyploids coexist with diploids in highly variable proportions among populations in Senegal. Acacia senegal genotypes were predominantly diploid and tetraploid, but triploid, pentaploid, hexaploid, and octaploid forms were also found. We find that polyploids show faster growth than diploids under our test conditions: in an 18 years old field trial, polyploid superiority was estimated to be 17% in trunk diameter and 9% in height while in a growth chamber experiment, polyploids grew 28% taller, but only after being exposed to drought stress. The results suggest that polyploid A. senegal can have an adaptive advantage in some regions of Africa.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Diallo, Adja M.","Nielsen, Lene R.","Kjær, Erik D.","Petersen, Karen Koefoed","Ræbild, Anders"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-015285"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Adaptation","Drought stress","Flow Cytometry","Microsatellite markers","Senegalia senegal"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"polyploidy can confer superiority to west african acacia senegal l willd trees","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2016.00821","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-015285","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4906048","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"27379120","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84975125306","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-24ff2892-2664-48b9-a474-049c5bc0f399","ku-3081613e-de96-4ef4-a0f4-f04412e3a7e9"],"keyword":["Microsatellite markers","Senegalia senegal","adaptation","arid zone trees","drought stress","flow cytometry","morphological differentiation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-8DA808A695DB35EDFAAA6D990BA395D5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2016.00821","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/162122354/Polyploidy_can_confer_superiority_to_West_African_Acacia_senegal_L._Willd._trees.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Food Science, Department of Food Science - Plant, Food & Climate"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Adja M.","key":"diallopau","last":"Diallo","name":"Diallo, Adja M.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene R.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Lene R.","orcid":"0000-0002-7214-8691","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Erik D.","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","name":"Kjær, Erik D.","orcid":"0000-0001-8624-1611","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Karen Koefoed","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"d66e86e4-c15c-4719-8e46-212a78c39858","name":"Petersen, Karen Koefoed","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"ræbildpau","last":"Ræbild","name":"Ræbild, Anders","orcid":"0000-0002-8793-5663","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27379120"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fpls.2016.00821","issn":["1664462X"],"issue":"JUNE2016","journal":"Frontiers in Plant Science","paperid":"821","publisher":"Frontiers Media SA","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Plant Science","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84975125306"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-24ff2892-2664-48b9-a474-049c5bc0f399","ku-3081613e-de96-4ef4-a0f4-f04412e3a7e9"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Polyploidy can Confer Superiority to West African Acacia senegal (L.) Willd. Trees","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-015285","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"This chapter focuses on two main issues: production of scientific knowledge and capacity building at African research universities and how these two subjects are interwoven. The global production of scientific knowledge has been contested by authors like Connell (2007), who describes how social science theory has formed itself not only on Western ethnocentric assumptions but also from a position of privilege claiming universal knowledge and universal values. Taking a somewhat broader perspective, Linda Tuhiwai Smith describes this as 'research through imperial eyes' (Smith, 2012: 44), giving a wide range of examples of its implications for our understanding of, for example, conceptions of the individual and society, as well as space and time. Research outside the Global North (e.g. Africa) has often been perceived as a place for collecting empirical data, while research in the Global North has been seen as a place for theory development (Hountondji, 1995). Against this background, this chapter also sees scientific knowledge as situated as described in Chapter 2 of this book.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Madsen, Lene Møller","Nielsen, Thomas Theis"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-040356"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"negotiating scientific knowledge about climate change enhancing research capacity through phd students","src":["ku","ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S02-100.00-040356","identifier":[{"id":"84960517096","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-13efd6d8-3957-4467-871a-82818b868d1d","ruc-617916cb-74e3-425a-8507-64945cb75d68"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-4-370E77105A5E9AAE9F6A0CDB62CB7570"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Science Education","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Science Education, Equity in Science Education","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Science Education, University Science Education"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology","Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology, Space, Place, Mobility and Urban Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Lene Møller","key":"madsenpau","last":"Madsen","locid":"dda148da-5b6d-4f4e-ab60-b7cf85637829","name":"Madsen, Lene Møller","orcid":"0000-0001-7149-9142","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","first":"Thomas Theis","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"7720df43-0a90-48d4-9d38-0667f3a11654","name":"Nielsen, Thomas Theis","orcid":"0000-0002-8134-3456","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Hanne Kirstine","key":"adriansenpedh","last":"Adriansen","name":"Adriansen, Hanne Kirstine","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Lene Møller","key":"madsenpedh","last":"Madsen","name":"Madsen, Lene Møller","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Stig","key":"jensenpedh","last":"Jensen","name":"Jensen, Stig","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4324/9781315734620-10","eisbn":["9781315734620"],"isbn":["9781138838154"],"pages":"147-168","place":"Abingdom","publisher":"Routledge","series":"Routledge Studies in African Development","status":"p","subtitle":"The geography and power of knowledge under changing conditions","title":"Higher Education and Capacity Building in Africa"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84960517096"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Roskilde University","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-13efd6d8-3957-4467-871a-82818b868d1d","ruc-617916cb-74e3-425a-8507-64945cb75d68"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Negotiating scientific knowledge about climate change: enhancing research capacity through PhD students","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-040356","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
