{"abstract":"This single-case study examined an arterio-venous shunt patient (20 year old male) where a main artery supplying the left leg in the deeper anterior pelvis was connected to a returning vein, possibly due to previous trauma received while playing high level Team Handball. Due to this A-V shunt muscle blood flow was transiently restricted in the left leg when the patient was exercising with his lower limbs (verified by ultrasonography imaging), which led to marked onset of muscle fatigue within 1-2 minutes of running activity. This pathological condition had evolved over a few years of continued sports activity, resulting in visible hypertrophy of his left leg. AIM: To study the effect of chronic blood-flow restricted (BFR) exercise conditions on skeletal muscle size and myogenic satellite cell (SC) expression in an arterio-venous shunt patient. METHODS: Muscle biopsies were obtained from the left (L; affected limb) and right (R) VL muscle, and examined for mean fiber area, quiescent (Pax7+) and activated SCs (MyoD+, Myogenin+, Ki67+) and myonuclei number using immuno-fluorescence techniques. RESULTS: Mean myofiber area was 38% greater in L vs. R (6729 ± 2382 vs 4898 ± 2050 µm2), while myonuclear content was 14% higher (4.2 ± 1.6 vs 3.7 ±1.3 myonuclei per myofiber), myonuclear domain was 22% larger (1807.0 ± 912.9 vs. 1483.9 ± 765.5 µm2 per myonuclei) and Pax7+ cells were 93% more abundant (0.189 ± 0.43 vs. 0.098 ± 0.297 Pax7+ cells per myofiber) in L compared to R. In addition, marked up regulation in MyoD+ (+266%), Myogenin+ (+31%) and Ki67+ (0.022 vs. 0.000 SCs per myofiber) positive SCs was observed in L vs R. DISCUSSION: Using between-limb comparisons the present single-case study demonstrate that chronic exercise-induced BFR due to arterio-venous shunting led to a marked up-regulation in myogenic satellite cell activity within all stages of the cell cycle, which was accompanied by substantial muscle hypertrophy. Specifically, muscle fiber cross-sectional area (40%) and myonuclei number (15%) were elevated in the affected leg, together with an elevated myonuclear domain (20%). This single-case study confirms previous result from our Lab demonstrating that blood-flow restricted muscle exercise leads to a marked activation of myogenic SCs, upregulated myonuclei number and marked myofiber hypertrophy.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Aagaard, Per","Jacobsen, Mikkel","Jensen, Kasper Y.","Nielsen, Jakob L.","Bülow, Jens","Rørdam, Lene","Suetta, Charlotte","Frandsen, Ulrik"],"cuid":["S02-094.97-040765"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Journal Article"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"effects of chronic blood flow restriction exercise on skeletal muscle size and myogenic satellite cell expression","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S02-094.97-040765","identifier":[{"id":"27362069","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-20928517-2ce3-4cfd-b4d2-3f6449c6af32","sdu-daa7849a-2461-4e28-9375-c80eb361d557"],"keyword":["Journal Article"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CF29825CCFD75330840E2A15702900E9"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics, Research Unit of Muscle Physiology and Biomechanics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Endocrinology and Metabolism"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Hvidovre Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Per","key":"aagaardpau","last":"Aagaard","locid":"8452f86e-3c45-4ecb-9e11-54c72d45d669","name":"Aagaard, Per","orcid":"0000-0002-9773-7361","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mikkel","key":"jacobsenpau","last":"Jacobsen","locid":"bc1f34d5-35f8-43a7-811e-2d3150b4d037","name":"Jacobsen, Mikkel","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper Yde","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"ea71177b-d7db-4b1c-820c-f93cdc00b99b","name":"Jensen, Kasper Yde","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jakob L","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"6ccdf2a1-ca2a-4797-a19b-ed6051f7f4b6","name":"Nielsen, Jakob Lindberg","name_org":"Nielsen, Jakob L","orcid":"0000-0003-0979-4647","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Jens","key":"bülowpau","last":"Bülow","locid":"0126f721-05d2-4ce9-8c25-20b41eb01f9a","name":"Bülow, Jens","orcid":"0000-0002-3921-4888","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"rørdampau","last":"Rørdam","name":"Rørdam, Lene","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Charlotte","key":"suettapau","last":"Suetta","locid":"26af20c6-3c1b-4b5a-aa41-bf3e1598e2b0","name":"Suetta, Charlotte","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ulrik","key":"frandsenpau","last":"Frandsen","locid":"cfa419b9-9116-4553-8bb9-363a443f07f2","name":"Frandsen, Ulrik","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27362069"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1249/01.mss.0000488106.09083.4f","issn":["01959131","15300315"],"issn_org":["01959131"],"issue":"5 (Suppl. 1)","journal":"Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise","pages":"1032-3","paperid":"3703","publisher":"Lippincott Williams and Wilkins","status":"p","title":"Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise","volume":"48"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-20928517-2ce3-4cfd-b4d2-3f6449c6af32","sdu-daa7849a-2461-4e28-9375-c80eb361d557"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Effects of Chronic Blood-Flow Restriction Exercise on Skeletal Muscle Size and Myogenic Satellite Cell Expression","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-094.97-040765","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: With the aging of the population living with HIV, the absolute risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) is increasing. There is a need to further facilitate the identification of persons at elevated risk in routine practice. METHODS AND RESULTS: Prospective information was collected on 32,663 HIV-positive persons from 20 countries in Europe and Australia, who were free of CVD at entry into the Data-collection on Adverse Effects of Anti-HIV Drugs (D:A:D) study. Cox regression models (full and reduced) were developed that predict the risk of a global CVD endpoint. The predictive performance of the D:A:D models were compared with a recent CVD prediction model from the Framingham study, which was assessed recalibrated to the D:A:D dataset. A total of 1010 CVD events occurred during 186,364.5 person-years. The full D:A:D CVD prediction model included age, gender, systolic blood pressure, smoking status, family history of CVD, diabetes, total cholesterol, high-density lipoprotein, CD4 lymphocyte count, cumulative exposure to protease- and nucleoside reverse transcriptase-inhibitors, and current use of abacavir. A reduced model omitted antiretroviral therapies. The D:A:D models statistically significantly predicted risk more accurately than the recalibrated Framingham model (Harrell's c-statistic of 0.791, 0.783 and 0.766 for the D:A:D full, D:A:D reduced, and Framingham models respectively; p < 0.001). The D:A:D models also more accurately predicted five-year CVD-risk for key prognostic subgroups. CONCLUSIONS: An updated, easily recalibrated, global CVD-risk equation tailored to HIV-positive persons was developed using routinely collected CVD risk parameters and incorporating markers on immune function (CD4 lymphocyte count), and exposure to antiretroviral therapies. The estimated CVD risk can be used to quantify risk and to guide preventive care.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Friis-Møller, Nina","Nielsen, Lene Ryom","Smith, Colette","Weber, Rainer","Reiss, Peter","Dabis, F","De Wit, Stephane","Monforte, Antonella D'Arminio","Kirk, Ole","Fontas, Eric","Sabin, Caroline","Phillips, Andrew","Lundgren, Jens","Law, Matthew"],"cuid":["S02-097.68-042642"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corpau":["D:A:D Study Group"],"keyword":["AIDS","Epidemiology","HIV"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an updated prediction model of the global risk of cardiovascular disease in hiv positive persons the data collection on adverse effects of anti hiv drugs d a d study","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2047487315579291","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-097.68-042642","identifier":[{"id":"25882821","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84953302216","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-5991f401-88d7-452c-a0a7-d9f04d261ebd","sdu-8ea518e4-758d-4aa7-8470-756f363925e7"],"keyword":["AIDS","CVD risk prediction","HIV","epidemiology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-93F1922064532EB09D630D68DDABB520"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2047487315579291","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Infectious Diseases (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":3,"key":"d a d study group","name":"D:A:D Study Group","role":"oau"}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nina","key":"friismøllerpau","last":"Friis-Møller","locid":"80cb1385-5014-4213-abcb-76ee11268ade","name":"Friis-Møller, Nina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lene Ryom","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Lene Ryom","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"ryompau","last":"Ryom","name":"Ryom, Lene","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Colette","key":"smithpau","last":"Smith","name":"Smith, Colette","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Rainer","key":"weberpau","last":"Weber","name":"Weber, Rainer","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Peter","key":"reisspau","last":"Reiss","name":"Reiss, Peter","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"F","key":"dabispau","last":"Dabis","name":"Dabis, F","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Stephane","key":"dewitpau","last":"De Wit","name":"De Wit, Stephane","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Antonella D'Arminio","key":"monfortepau","last":"Monforte","name":"Monforte, Antonella D'Arminio","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Ole","key":"kirkpau","last":"Kirk","locid":"67bce469-8913-44ac-8d6d-7dff1171c771","name":"Kirk, Ole","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Eric","key":"fontaspau","last":"Fontas","name":"Fontas, Eric","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Caroline","key":"sabinpau","last":"Sabin","name":"Sabin, Caroline","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Andrew","key":"phillipspau","last":"Phillips","name":"Phillips, Andrew","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jens","key":"lundgrenpau","last":"Lundgren","locid":"af310f7a-c49b-4381-bcb4-cf56e3fd80d8","name":"Lundgren, Jens","orcid":"0000-0001-8901-7850","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Matthew","key":"lawpau","last":"Law","name":"Law, Matthew","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25882821"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/2047487315579291","issn":["20474873","20474881"],"issn_org":["20474873"],"issue":"2","journal":"European Journal of Preventive Cardiology","pages":"214-223","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"European Journal of Preventive Cardiology","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84953302216"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-5991f401-88d7-452c-a0a7-d9f04d261ebd","sdu-8ea518e4-758d-4aa7-8470-756f363925e7"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"An updated prediction model of the global risk of cardiovascular disease in HIV-positive persons : The Data-collection on Adverse Effects of Anti-HIV Drugs (D:A:D) study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-097.68-042642","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"In recent years, several genes have been causally associated with epilepsy. However, making a genetic diagnosis in a patient can still be difficult, since extensive phenotypic and genetic heterogeneity has been observed in many monogenic epilepsies. This study aimed to analyze the genetic basis of a wide spectrum of epilepsies with age of onset spanning from the neonatal period to adulthood. A gene panel targeting 46 epilepsy genes was used on a cohort of 216 patients consecutively referred for panel testing. The patients had a range of different epilepsies from benign neonatal seizures to epileptic encephalopathies (EEs). Potentially causative variants were evaluated by literature and database searches, submitted to bioinformatic prediction algorithms, and validated by Sanger sequencing. If possible, parents were included for segregation analysis. We identified a presumed disease-causing variant in 49 (23%) of the 216 patients. The variants were found in 19 different genes including SCN1A, STXBP1, CDKL5, SCN2A, SCN8A, GABRA1, KCNA2, and STX1B. Patients with neonatal-onset epilepsies had the highest rate of positive findings (57%). The overall yield for patients with EEs was 32%, compared to 17% among patients with generalized epilepsies and 16% in patients with focal or multifocal epilepsies. By the use of a gene panel consisting of 46 epilepsy genes, we were able to find a disease-causing genetic variation in 23% of the analyzed patients. The highest yield was found among patients with neonatal-onset epilepsies and EEs.","author_count":37,"contributors":["Møller, Rikke S","Larsen, Line H G","Johannesen, Katrine M","Talvik, Inga","Talvik, Tiina","Vaher, Ulvi","Miranda, Maria J","Farooq, Muhammad","Nielsen, Jens E K","Svendsen, Lene Lavard","Kjelgaard, Ditte B","Linnet, Karen Markussen","Hao, Qin","Uldall, Peter","Frangu, Mimoza","Tommerup, Niels","Baig, Shahid M","Abdullah, Uzma","Born, Alfred P","Gellert, Pia","Nikanorova, Marina","Olofsson, Kern","Jepsen, Birgit","Marjanovic, Dragan","Al-Zehhawi, Lana I K","Peñalva, Sofia J","Krag-Olsen, Bente","Brusgaard, Klaus","Hjalgrim, Helle","Rubboli, Guido","Pal, Deb K","Dahl, Hans A"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-028709"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, South","Asia, South","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Argentina","Estonia","Pakistan","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CEMIC: Center for Medical Education and Clinical Research","King's College London","National Institute for Biotechnology and Genetic Engineering (NIBGE)-PIEAS","Tallinn Children’s Hospital","Tartu University Clinic","University of Tartu"],"keyword":["Epileptic encephalopathies","Familial epilepsies","Seizures"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-FILA","DI-HO-ZEA"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-FILA","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"gene panel testing in epileptic encephalopathies and familial epilepsies","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5073625/pdf/msy-0007-0210.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S03-100.00-028709","identifier":[{"id":"PMC5073625","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"27781031","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84983748486","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-dd314d09-2b25-429d-af98-529df3543826","ku-c6f6d84c-b9df-437a-a5cf-d5858a6536f3","sdu-0316c54c-130b-46cf-ae1b-7d1cee865485"],"keyword":["Epileptic encephalopathies","Familial epilepsies","Gene panel testing","Seizures"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientif