 Gut Health, and Fermentation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"novonesis","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-NOSI","l3nn":"Novonesis","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Novonesis","namevar":["Chr. Hansen"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-NOSI","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"bf","key":"département technologie alimentaire irsat cnrst","loc":["Burkina Faso","Africa"],"name":"Département Technologie Alimentaire/IRSAT/CNRST","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"David Bichala","key":"adimpongpau","last":"Adimpong","locid":"4db0ae2e-8bbf-4026-8e4d-cdd58029a5b0","name":"Adimpong, David Bichala","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Dennis Sandris","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"08539a0e-05f4-454e-ad2a-de195363dbd1","name":"Nielsen, Dennis Sandris","orcid":"0000-0001-8121-1114","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Kim I.","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Kim I.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Finn Kvist","key":"vogensenpau","last":"Vogensen","locid":"337bf47b-655e-4e27-a5e0-04dd672a0876","name":"Vogensen, Finn Kvist","orcid":"0000-0003-2151-7802","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Hagrétou","key":"sawadogolinganipau","last":"Sawadogo-Lingani","name":"Sawadogo-Lingani, Hagrétou","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Patrick M.F.","key":"derkxpau","last":"Derkx","name":"Derkx, Patrick M.F.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lene","key":"jespersenpau","last":"Jespersen","locid":"a156c6e3-9562-4d35-9305-53a39c62e076","name":"Jespersen, Lene","orcid":"0000-0002-2555-5948","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1099/ijs.0.048769-0","issn":["10706259","14665026"],"issn_org":["14665026"],"issue":"11","journal":"International Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology","pages":"3720-3726","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology","volume":"63"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84884839440"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Private Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-6b947760-32b4-418d-a71b-14a73006b7d0"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"<em>Lactobacillus delbrueckii </em>subsp. <em>jakobsenii</em> subsp. nov., isolated from dolo wort, an alcoholic fermented beverage in Burkina Faso","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-457692","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Bacteriophage λ stably maintains its dormant prophage state but efficiently enters lytic development in response to DNA damage. The mediator of these processes is the λ repressor protein, CI, and its interactions with λ operator DNA. This λ switch is a model on the basis of which epigenetic switch regulation is understood. Using single molecule analysis, we directly examined the stability of the CI-operator structure in its natural, supercoiled state. We marked positions adjacent to the λ operators with peptide nucleic acids and monitored their movement by tethered particle tracking. Compared with relaxed DNA, the presence of supercoils greatly enhances juxtaposition probability. Also, the efficiency and cooperativity of the λ switch is significantly increased in the supercoiled system compared with a linear assay, increasing the Hill coefficient.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Norregaard, Kamilla","Andersson, Magnus","Sneppen, Kim","Nielsen, Peter E.","Brown, Stanley","Oddershede, Lene Broeng"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-457879"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Algorithms","DNA Viral","Epigenesis Genetic","Lysogeny","Models Genetic","Prophages","Protein Binding","Repressor Proteins","Thermodynamics","Virus Activation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dna supercoiling enhances cooperativity and efficiency of an epigenetic switch","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1215907110","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-457879","identifier":[{"id":"24101469","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC3808602","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"84886377071","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000325943300053","type":"wos"}],"ids":["ku-6be20c0c-e60b-40dc-980a-ad31041ef6da"],"keyword":["Algorithms","Bacteriophage lambda","DNA, Superhelical","DNA, Viral","Epigenesis, Genetic","Lysogeny","Models, Genetic","Operator Regions, Genetic","Prophages","Protein Binding","Repressor Proteins","Thermodynamics","Viral Regulatory and Accessory Proteins","Virus Activation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C85DB43B57DE63A030E2D0439658A7DF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1215907110","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Niels Bohr Institute, Biocomplexity"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Kamilla","key":"norregaardpau","last":"Norregaard","locid":"c34c6d45-ccd4-4be4-9728-7f492a6b4583","name":"Norregaard, Kamilla","orcid":"0000-0003-1953-9703","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Magnus","key":"anderssonpau","last":"Andersson","name":"Andersson, Magnus","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kim","key":"sneppenpau","last":"Sneppen","locid":"50bdf992-9fe4-4a9e-ba66-909b7e259a51","name":"Sneppen, Kim","orcid":"0000-0001-9820-3567","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Peter Eigil","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"74cb6e41-3bfc-4d0a-a0df-7bd8fa9c2856","name":"Nielsen, Peter E.","name_org":"Nielsen, Peter Eigil","orcid":"0000-0001-8771-330X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Stanley","key":"brownpau","last":"Brown","locid":"fe5af114-5058-406e-87f5-18be42ae3241","name":"Brown, Stanley","orcid":"0000-0002-8453-8144","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lene B","key":"oddershedepau","last":"Oddershede","locid":"7474640c-fb11-45b8-abd4-8de4232d5060","name":"Oddershede, Lene Broeng","name_org":"Oddershede, Lene B","orcid":"0000-0003-2923-2844","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24101469"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1073/pnas.1215907110","issn":["00278424","10916490"],"issn_org":["00278424"],"issue":"43","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","pages":"17386-91","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","title_org":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences USA (PNAS)","volume":"110"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84886377071"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-6be20c0c-e60b-40dc-980a-ad31041ef6da"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"DNA supercoiling enhances cooperativity and efficiency of an epigenetic switch","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-457879","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Gap junctions are comprised of connexins that form cell-to-cell channels which couple neighboring cells to accommodate the exchange of information. The need for communication does, however, change over time and therefore must be tightly controlled. Although the regulation of connexin protein expression by transcription and translation is of great importance, the trafficking, channel activity and degradation are also under tight control. The function of connexins can be regulated by several post translational modifications, which affect numerous parameters; including number of channels, open probability, single channel conductance or selectivity. The most extensively investigated post translational modifications are phosphorylations, which have been documented in all mammalian connexins. Besides phosphorylations, some connexins are known to be ubiquitinated, SUMOylated, nitrosylated, hydroxylated, acetylated, methylated, and γ-carboxyglutamated. The aim of the present review is to summarize our current knowledge of post translational regulation of the connexin family of proteins.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Axelsen, Lene Nygaard","Calloe, Kirstine","von Holstein-Rathlou, Niels-Henrik","Nielsen, Morten Schak"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-494513"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"managing the complexity of communication regulation of gap junctions by post translational modification","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2013.00130","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-494513","identifier":[{"id":"PMC3804956","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"24155720","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-a939fbd5-7b7f-4df6-8403-3cf5c63e0ec9"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-5A88321646900794EF22951537C07B7E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2013.00130","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Renal and Vascular Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Lene Nygaard","key":"axelsenpau","last":"Axelsen","locid":"2632f623-a6d6-49e1-884c-0560715787be","name":"Axelsen, Lene Nygaard","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kirstine","key":"calløpau","last":"Callø","locid":"9fc39d74-d0ac-4774-b837-de97103c87f0","name":"Calloe, Kirstine","name_org":"Callø, Kirstine","orcid":"0000-0002-1777-7938","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Niels-Henrik","key":"vonholsteinrathloupau","last":"von Holstein-Rathlou","locid":"6476af94-cb91-43db-bc71-5f81cd3d5827","name":"von Holstein-Rathlou, Niels-Henrik","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Morten S","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"b203113e-fe9b-4863-8eb0-3022ca8cc9e2","name":"Nielsen, Morten Schak","name_org":"Nielsen, Morten S","orcid":"0000-0002-9718-5421","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24155720"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fphar.2013.00130","issn":["16639812"],"journal":"Frontiers in Pharmacology","pages":"1-18","paperid":"130","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Pharmacology","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-a939fbd5-7b7f-4df6-8403-3cf5c63e0ec9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Managing the complexity of communication: regulation of gap junctions by post-translational modification","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-494513","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Strongyle parasites are ubiquitous in grazing horses and constitute a potential threat to equine health. Feces were collected from six horses four times daily over a period of 5 days. Fecal egg counts (FECs) were performed to identify any diurnal rhythms in strongyle egg shedding and to quantify variability at the different levels: individual horses, repeated counts, repeated subsamples, different time points, and different days. No significant differences in FECs were found between the different time points (P = .11). The variables-horse, day, subsample, and egg count-accounted for a variance of 104.83, 0.10, 7.24, and 5.61, respectively. The apparent lack of additional variability between the four different time points suggests that time of the day chosen for collecting fecal samples does not constitute a source of error in field studies. The majority of variability exists between different subsamples and repeated egg counts on the same subsamples, whereas the variability of FECs between following days can be considered negligible. The findings of this study have implication for designing and performing field surveillance of strongyle FEC levels and applying the FEC reduction test for evaluating anthelmintic efficacy. (C) 2013 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Carstensen, Helena","Larsen, Lene","Ritz, Christian","Nielsen, Martin Krarup"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-501167"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Horse","McMaster","Strongyles","Variability"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"daily variability of strongyle fecal egg counts in horses","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jevs.2012.06.001","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-501167","identifier":[{"id":"84874294551","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-b460dc38-1fb5-4326-aaca-36cdd4775a88"],"keyword":["Fecal egg count","Horse","McMaster","Strongyles","Variability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BAC83AAEA4C4D8ED7D21840CB3B3D6A7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jevs.2012.06.001","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences, Section for Medicine and Surgery"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Helena","key":"carstensenpau","last":"Carstensen","locid":"23a80c36-171e-427a-b5fa-8af5e9cc5cfb","name":"Carstensen, Helena","orcid":"0000-0001-8952-9637","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lene","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Lene","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Christian","key":"ritzpau","last":"Ritz","locid":"c881424f-d529-443e-b60e-bc2edb674ede","name":"Ritz, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-5095-0624","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Martin K.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"e55baed5-a4d1-42d4-b5ad-a86d9097837f","name":"Nielsen, Martin Krarup","name_org":"Nielsen, Martin K.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jevs.2012.06.001","issn":["07370806","15427412"],"issn_org":["07370806"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of Equine Veterinary Science","pages":"161-164","status":"p","title":"Journal of Equine Veterinary Science","volume":"33"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84874294551"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-b460dc38-1fb5-4326-aaca-36cdd4775a88"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Daily variability of strongyle fecal egg counts in horses","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-501167","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVETwins in Africa may be at increased risk of metabolic disorders due to strained conditions in utero, including high exposure to infections. We studied metabolic syndrome (MS) and diabetes mellitus (DM) among young twins and singletons in Guinea-Bissau.RESEARCH DESIGN AND METHODSThe study was cross-sectional and occurred from October 2009 until August 2011 at the Bandim Health Project, a demographic surveillance site in the capital Bissau. Twins and singleton controls between 5 and 32 years were visited at home. Fasting blood samples for metabolic measurements were collected. Zygosity was established genetically for a subset. DM was defined as HbA1c ≥6.5% (48 mmol/mol) and MS by the International Diabetes Federation criteria.RESULTSHbA1c was available for 574 twins and 463 singletons. Mean age was 15.3 years versus 15.8 years, respectively. Eighteen percent of twins were monozygotic. There were no DM cases among twins but one among singletons. A total of 1.4% (8 of 574) of twins had elevated HbA1c (6.0-6.4%, 42-46 mmol/mol) compared with 2.4% (11 of 463) of singletons (P = 0.28). Mean HbA1c was 5.3% (34 mmol/mol) for both groups. MS data were available for 364 twins and 360 singletons. The MS prevalence was 3.0% (11 of 364) among twins and 3.6% (13 of 360) among singletons (P = 0.66). The prevalence of fasting blood glucose (F-glucose) ≥5.6 mmol/L was 34.9% (127 of 364) for twins versus 24.7% (89 of 360) for singletons (P = 0.003). Median homeostasis model assessment insulin resistance did not differ (P = 0.34).CONCLUSIONSThe MS and DM prevalences among young individuals in Guinea-Bissau were low. Twins did not have a higher MS and DM burden than singletons, though elevated F-glucose was more common among twins.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Bjerregaard-Andersen, Morten","Hansen, Lone","da Silva, Leontina I","Joaquím, Luis C","Egegaard Hennild, Ditte","Christiansen, Lene","Aaby, Peter","Benn, Christine Stabell","Christensen, Kaare","Sodemann, Morten","Jensen, Dorte Møller","Beck-Nielsen, Henning"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-590712"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"risk of metabolic syndrome and diabetes among young twins and singletons in guinea bissau","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://europepmc.org/abstract/med/23949562","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-590712","identifier":[{"id":"PMC3816924","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC3816924","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"23949562","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84891888017","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-3ceffef7-b3fa-4870-ac21-211eb6d5e734"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-713EB5A0711B4EDF386D76FF83665140"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Clinical Biochemistry (Odense)","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Clinical Genetics (Odense)","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Epidemiology, Biostatistics and Biodemography","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, The Danish Twin Registry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten","key":"bjerrega