{"abstract":"Klebsiella pneumoniae is an important opportunistic pathogen causing pneumonia, sepsis and urinary tract infections. Colonisation of the gastrointestinal (GI) tract is a key step in the development of infections; yet the specific factors important for K. pneumoniae to colonize and reside in the GI tract of the host are largely unknown. To identify K. pneumoniae genes promoting GI colonisation, a novel genomic-library-based approach was employed.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Boll, Erik J.","Nielsen, Lene Nørby","Krogfelt, Karen A.","Struve, Carsten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-439045"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Former LIFE faculty","Klebsiella pneumoniae"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"novel screening assay for em in vivo em selection of em klebsiella pneumoniae em genes promoting gastrointestinal colonisation","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2180-12-201","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-439045","identifier":[{"id":"22967317","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84865974329","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC3463446","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-4c7da328-328f-41a2-ad7a-7abe4f1896a3"],"keyword":["Former LIFE faculty","Genomic library","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Mouse model of gastrointestinal colonisation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6A2B717818A0800305A1BF89E04BFC21"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2180-12-201","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ssi statens serum institut","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-SSI","l3nn":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","namevar":["Statens Serum Institut"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-SSI","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Life Sciences, Department of Veterinary Disease Biology, Section of Microbiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Erik J.","key":"bollpau","last":"Boll","name":"Boll, Erik J.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Lene N","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"1a4f890a-d171-4c2f-ae00-dd1e024c25ab","name":"Nielsen, Lene Nørby","name_org":"Nielsen, Lene N","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Karen A.","key":"krogfeltpau","last":"Krogfelt","name":"Krogfelt, Karen A.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Carsten","key":"struvepau","last":"Struve","name":"Struve, Carsten","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["22967317"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/1471-2180-12-201","issn":["14712180"],"issue":"201","journal":"BMC Microbiology","status":"p","title":"BMC Microbiology","title_org":"B M C Microbiology","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84865974329"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4c7da328-328f-41a2-ad7a-7abe4f1896a3"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Novel screening assay for<em> in vivo</em> selection of <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em> genes promoting gastrointestinal colonisation","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-439045","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"Cell-penetrating peptides (CPPs) provide a promising approach for enhancing intracellular delivery of therapeutic biomacromolecules by increasing transport through membrane barriers. Here, proteolytically stable cell-penetrating peptidomimetics with α-peptide/β-peptoid backbone were studied to evaluate the effect of α-chirality in the β-peptoid residues and the presence of guanidinium groups in the α-amino acid residues on membrane interaction. The molecular properties of the peptidomimetics in solution (surface and intramolecular hydrogen bonding, aqueous diffusion rate and molecular size) were studied along with their adsorption to lipid bilayers, cellular uptake, and toxicity. The surface hydrogen bonding ability of the peptidomimetics reflected their adsorbed amounts onto lipid bilayers as well as with their cellular uptake, indicating the importance of hydrogen bonding for their membrane interaction and cellular uptake. Ellipsometry studies further demonstrated that the presence of chiral centers in the β-peptoid residues promotes a higher adsorption to anionic lipid bilayers, whereas circular dichroism results showed that α-chirality influences their overall mean residue ellipticity. The presence of guanidinium groups and α-chiral β-peptoid residues was also found to have a significant positive effect on uptake in living cells. Together, the findings provide an improved understanding on the behavior of cell-penetrating peptidomimetics in the presence of lipid bilayers and live cells.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Jing, Xiaona","Yang, Mingjun","Kasimova, Marina Robertovna","Malmsten, Martin","Franzyk, Henrik","Jørgensen, Lene","Foged, Camilla","Nielsen, Hanne Mørck"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-444969"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Uppsala University"],"keyword":["Adsorption","Calorimetry","Circular Dichroism","HeLa Cells","Humans","Hydrogen bonding","Liposomes","Models Molecular","Molecular Dynamics Simulation","Peptidomimetics","Peptoids","Protein Binding","Stereoisomerism"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"membrane adsorption and binding cellular uptake and cytotoxicity of cell penetrating peptidomimetics with α peptide β peptoid backbone effects of hydrogen bonding and α chirality in the β peptoid residues","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bbamem.2012.05.003","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-444969","identifier":[{"id":"84864065351","type":"scopus"},{"id":"22609348","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-563e4516-b3c3-4690-a9cc-41ba300e6aaf"],"keyword":["Adsorption","Calorimetry","Circular Dichroism","HeLa Cells","Humans","Hydrogen Bonding","Liposomes","Models, Molecular","Molecular Dynamics Simulation","Peptidomimetics","Peptoids","Protein Binding","Stereoisomerism"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7EC8C5B113E49F307E93593451DCD6AB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bbamem.2012.05.003","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2012","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Chemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"uppsala university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, E