ecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Iben Margrete","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"7af020b5-3cb6-48cf-9002-177bc56b36f3","name":"Thomsen, Iben Margrete","orcid":"0000-0001-7139-018X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Erik Dahl","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"e1bdaffb-0384-4159-aa20-a913a2cee948","name":"Kjær, Erik Dahl","orcid":"0000-0001-8624-1611","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["18155103"],"journal":"Forstschutz Aktuell","pages":"64-65","status":"p","title":"Forstschutz Aktuell","volume":"55"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-bd16b156-6116-4c05-b197-7f476c5479b9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Genetic variation in susceptibility to ash dieback","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-506311","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"Human exonuclease 1 (hEXO1) acts directly in diverse DNA processing events, including replication, mismatch repair (MMR), and double strand break repair (DSBR), and it was also recently described to function as damage sensor and apoptosis inducer following DNA damage. In contrast, 14-3-3 proteins are regulatory phosphorserine/threonine binding proteins involved in the control of diverse cellular events, including cell cycle checkpoint and apoptosis signaling. hEXO1 is regulated by post-translation Ser/Thr phosphorylation in a yet not fully clarified manner, but evidently three phosphorylation sites are specifically induced by replication inhibition leading to protein ubiquitination and degradation. We demonstrate direct and robust interaction between hEXO1 and six of the seven 14-3-3 isoforms in vitro, suggestive of a novel protein interaction network between DNA repair and cell cycle control. Binding experiments reveal weak affinity of the more selective isoform 14-3-3 sigma but both 14-3-3 isoforms eta and sigma significantly stimulate hEXO1 activity, indicating that these regulatory proteins exert a common regulation mode on hEXO1. Results demonstrate that binding involves the phosphorable amino acid S746 in hEXO1 and most likely a second unidentified binding motif. 14-3-3 associations do not appear to directly influence hEXO1 in vitro nuclease activity or in vitro DNA replication initiation. Moreover, specific phosphorylation variants, including hEXO1 S746A, are efficiently imported to the nucleus; to associate with PCNA in distinct replication foci and respond to DNA double strand breaks (DSBs), indicating that 14-3-3 binding does not involve regulating the subcellular distribution of hEXO1. Altogether, these results suggest that association may be related to regulation of hEXO1 availability during the DNA damage response to plausibly prevent extensive DNA resection at the damage site, as supported by recent studies","author_count":14,"contributors":["Andersen, Sofie Dabros","Keijzers, Guido","Rampakakis, Emmanouil","Engels, Kim","Luhn, Patricia","El-Shemerly, Mahmoud","Nielsen, Finn Cilius","Du, Yuhong","May, Alfred","Bohr, Vilhelm A.","Ferrari, Stefano","Zannis-Hadjopoulos, Maria","Fu, Haian","Rasmussen, Lene Juel"],"cuid":["S02-098.58-045656"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Emory University","University of Zurich"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"000000000014 000000000003 000000000003 checkpoint regulatory proteins interact specifically with dna repair protein human exonuclease 000000000001 hexo000000000001 via a semi conserved motif","src":["ku","ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dnarep.2011.11.007","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-098.58-045656","identifier":[{"id":"84856560000","type":"scopus"},{"id":"22222486","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC4586177","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-b96763cb-f50c-4583-9b38-c32f426c8109","ruc-67663c62-75ec-495e-9446-072a40584993"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-F047C6D8CA8A7F86BF033F1455EC1ACA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dnarep.2011.11.007","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","